A '''solar cell,''' also known as a '''photovoltaic cell''' ('''PV cell'''), is an electronic device that converts the energy of [[light]] directly into [[electricity]] by using the [[photovoltaic effect]].<ref name="chemistryexplained.com">[http://www.chemistryexplained.com/Ru-Sp/Solar-Cells.html Solar Cells]. chemistryexplained.com</ref> It is a type of photoelectric cell, a device whose electrical characteristics (such as [[Electric current|current]], [[voltage]], or [[Electrical resistance and conductance|resistance]]) vary when it is exposed to light. Individual solar cell devices are often the electrical building blocks of [[solar panel|photovoltaic modules]], known colloquially as "solar panels". Almost all commercial PV cells consist of [[crystalline silicon]], with a market share of 95%. Thin-film technologies account for a small share of the global photovoltaic module production. [[Cadmium telluride]] [[thin-film solar cell]]s (CdTe) is the dominant thin-film technology by production volume, while other thin-film technologies include copper indium gallium selenide (CIGS), and amorphous thin-film silicon.<ref>{{Cite report |url=https://iea-pvps.org/trends_reports/trends-2025/ |title=Trends in PV Applications 2025 |last=Masson |first=Gaëtan |last2=l’Epine |first2=Melodie de |last3=Kaizuka |first3=Izumi |last4=Okazaki |first4=Junko |date=2025-10-14 |publisher=IEA PVPS |language=en}}</ref> The common single-junction [[silicon]] solar cell can produce a maximum [[open-circuit voltage]] of approximately 0.5 to 0.6 [[volt]]s.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://solarbotics.net/starting/200202_solar_cells/200202_solar_cell_use.html|title=Solar cells – performance and →use|website=solarbotic s.net}}</ref>

[[Photovoltaic]] cells may operate under [[sunlight]] or artificial light. In addition to producing [[solar power]], they can be used as a [[photodetector]] (for example [[infrared detector]]s), to detect light or other [[electromagnetic radiation]] near the visible light range, as well as to measure light intensity.

The operation of a PV cell requires three basic attributes:
* The absorption of light, generating [[exciton]]s ([[bound state|bound]] [[electron]]-[[Electron hole|hole]] pairs), unbound electron-hole pairs (via excitons), or [[plasmons]].
* The separation of [[charge carrier]]s of opposite types.
* The separate extraction of those carriers to an external circuit.

There are multiple input factors that affect the output power of solar cells, such as [[temperature]], material properties, weather conditions, [[solar irradiance]] and more.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Al-Ezzi |first1=Athil S. |last2=Ansari |first2=Mohamed Nainar M. |date=2022-07-08 |title=Photovoltaic Solar Cells: A Review |journal=Applied System Innovation |language=en |volume=5 |issue=4 |page=67 |doi=10.3390/asi5040067 |issn=2571-5577 |doi-access=free}}</ref>

A similar type of "photoelectrolytic cell" ([[photoelectrochemical cell]]), can refer to devices:

* using light to excite electrons that can further be transported by a semiconductor which delivers the energy (like that explored by [[Edmond Becquerel]] and implemented in modern [[dye-sensitized solar cell]]s)
* using light to [[Water splitting|split water]] directly into [[hydrogen]] and [[oxygen]] which can further be used in power generation.

In contrast to outputting power directly, a [[solar thermal collector]] [[Absorption (electromagnetic radiation)|absorbs sunlight]], to produce either:

* direct heat as a "solar thermal module" or "solar hot water panel"
* indirect heat to be used to spin turbines in [[electrical power generation]].

Arrays of solar cells are used to make [[Solar panel|solar modules]] that generate a usable amount of [[direct current]] (DC) from [[sunlight]]. Strings of solar modules create a solar array to generate [[solar power]] using [[solar energy]], many times using an [[inverter]] to convert the solar power to [[alternating current]] (AC).{{TOC_limit|3}}

== Applications ==
=== Vehicular applications ===
[[File:GM_Sunraycer.JPG|thumb|The [[Sunraycer]] vehicle manufactured by [[General Motors]], [[AeroVironment]] and [[Hughes Aircraft]]]]

Electric vehicles that operate off of [[solar energy]] or sunlight are commonly referred to as solar cars.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Messenger |first1=Roger A. |title=Photovoltaic Systems Engineering |last2=Ventre |first2=Jerry |publisher=CRC Press |year=2010 |pages=455–460}}</ref> These vehicles use [[solar panel]]s to convert absorbed light into electrical energy to be used by electric motors, with any excess energy stored in [[Electric battery|batteries]].<ref name="Arulious-2021">{{Cite journal |last1=Arulious |first1=Jora A |last2=Earlina |first2=D |last3=Harish |first3=D |last4=Sakthi Priya |first4=P |last5=Inba Rexy |first5=A |last6=Nancy Mary |first6=J S |date=2021-11-01 |title=Design of solar powered electric vehicle |journal=Journal of Physics: Conference Series |volume=2070 |issue=1 |article-number=012105 |bibcode=2021JPhCS2070a2105A |doi=10.1088/1742-6596/2070/1/012105 |issn=1742-6588 |doi-access=free}}</ref> Batteries in solar-powered vehicles differ from starting batteries in standard [[Internal combustion engine|ICE]] cars because they are fashioned to impart power towards electrical components of the vehicle for a long durations.<ref>{{Cite web |last=US EPA |first=OAR |date=2021-05-14 |title=Electric Vehicle Myths |url=https://www.epa.gov/greenvehicles/electric-vehicle-myths |access-date=2025-05-31 |website=[[United States Environmental Protection Agency]] |language=en}}</ref>

The first instance of photovoltaic cells within vehicular applications was around midway through the second half of the 1900s. In an effort to increase publicity and awareness in solar powered transportation Hans Tholstrup decided to set up the first edition of the [[World Solar Challenge]] in 1987.{{cn|date=May 2026}} It was a 3000&nbsp;km race across the Australian outback where competitors from industry research groups and top universities around the globe were invited to compete. [[General Motors]] ended up winning the event by a significant margin with their [[Sunraycer]] vehicle that achieved speeds of over 40&nbsp;mph. Contrary to popular belief, solar powered cars are one of the oldest alternative energy vehicles.<ref>{{Cite conference |location=Capri, Italy |last=Connors |first=John |date=21-23 May 2007 |title=On the Subject of Solar Vehicles and the Benefits of the Technology |book-title=2007 International Conference on Clean Electrical Power |pages=700–705 |doi=10.1109/ICCEP.2007.384287}}</ref>

=== Cells, modules, panels and systems ===
{{Main|Solar panel|Photovoltaic system}}
[[File:From a solar cell to a PV system.svg|thumb|upright=1.35|From a solar cell to a PV system. Diagram of the possible components of a [[photovoltaic system]]]]
[[File:Dji fly 20230602 13744 PM 25 1719032157524 photo optimized.jpg|300px|thumb|Greencap Energy rooftop solar panels in [[Worthing]], [[United Kingdom]]]]

Multiple solar cells in an integrated group, all oriented in one plane, constitute a [[Solar panel|solar photovoltaic panel or module]]. Photovoltaic modules often have a sheet of glass on the sun-facing side, allowing light to pass while protecting the semiconductor [[Wafer (electronics)|wafers]]. Solar cells connected in [[series and parallel circuits|series]] creates an additive higher voltage, while connecting in parallel yields an additive higher current.

Parallel cells without bypass or shunt diodes that experience shade can shut down the weaker (less illuminated) parallel string (each string a number of series connected cells) causing substantial power loss and possible damage because of the reverse bias applied to the shaded cells by their illuminated partners.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Özkalay |first1=Ebrar |last2=Valoti |first2=Flavio |last3=Caccivio |first3=Mauro |last4=Virtuani |first4=Alessandro |last5=Friesen |first5=Gabi |last6=Ballif |first6=Christophe |date=2024 |title=The effect of partial shading on the reliability of photovoltaic modules in the built-environment |url=https://www.epj-pv.org/articles/epjpv/abs/2024/01/pv230068/pv230068.html |journal=EPJ Photovoltaics |language=en |volume=15 |page=7 |doi=10.1051/epjpv/2024001 |bibcode=2024EPJPv..15....7O |issn=2105-0716}}</ref>

Solar modules can be interconnected to create an array with a desired peak DC voltage and loading current capacity. This functionality can also be accomplished with various other solar devices that do more than just create the desired voltages and currents, such as with MPPTs ([[Maximum power point tracking|maximum power point trackers]]) or module level power electronic (MLPE) units: [[Solar micro-inverter|microinverters]] or [[Power optimizer#Isolating panels|DC-DC optimizers]].

Multiple solar cells assembled together in a single plane form a solar photovoltaic (PV) panel or module. These modules typically feature a glass sheet on the sun-facing side, which allows sunlight to pass through while safeguarding the semiconductor wafers from environmental factors. Connecting solar cells in series increases the voltage output, whereas parallel connections enhance the current output.<ref>{{cite web |title=Shading losses in PV systems, and techniques to mitigate them | date=17 October 2023 |url=https://aurorasolar.com/blog/shading-losses-in-pv-systems-and-techniques-to-mitigate-them/ |publisher=aurora |access-date=28 March 2025}}</ref>

Solar modules are often equipped with bypass diodes that isolate shaded cells, preventing them from affecting the performance of the entire string. These diodes allow the current to bypass the shaded or underperforming cells, thereby minimizing power loss and reducing the risk of damage.<ref>{{cite web |title=Shading |url=https://www.pveducation.org/pvcdrom/modules-and-arrays/shading |publisher=PVEducation |access-date=28 March 2025}}</ref>

