|page=28
}}
</ref>

[[Swanson's law]] is an observation similar to [[Moore's Law]] that states that solar cell prices fall 20% for every doubling of industry capacity. It was featured in an article in the British weekly newspaper [[The Economist]] in late 2012.<ref name="Econ1">{{cite news|url=https://www.economist.com/news/21566414-alternative-energy-will-no-longer-be-alternative-sunny-uplands| title =Sunny Uplands: Alternative energy will no longer be alternative| newspaper=The Economist| access-date = 28 December 2012| date=21 November 2012}}</ref> [[Balance of system]] costs are now higher than the solar panels alone, where in 2018 commercial arrays could be built at below $1.00 a watt, fully commissioned.<ref name="nrel-june2021" />

Over decades, costs for solar cells and panels has declined for many reasons:

# Larger boules. When the semiconductor industry moved to ever-larger [[Boule (crystal)|boules]], older equipment became inexpensive.
# Larger physical solar cell size. Sizes grew as surplus semiconductor equipment became available. [[ARCO]] Solar's original panels used cells {{convert|2|to|4|in|mm|-1}} in diameter. Panels in the 1990s and early 2000s generally used 125&nbsp;mm wafers; since 2008, almost all new panels use greater than 156mm cells,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2019-12-13 |title=From M0 to M12 - different wafer sizes in the market |url=https://blog.ibc-solar.com/2019/12/from-m0-to-m12-different-wafer-sizes-in-the-market/ |access-date=2024-10-28 |website=IBC SOLAR Blog |language=en-US}}</ref> and by 2020 even larger 182mm 'M10' cells.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bhambhani |first=Anu |date=2020-06-25 |title=Group Proposes M10 As New Silicon Wafer Standard Size |url=https://taiyangnews.info/technology/group-proposes-m10-as-new-silicon-wafer-standard-size |access-date=2024-10-28 |website=TaiyangNews − All About Solar Power |language=en}}</ref>
# Availability of large, high-quality glass sheets to cover the panels. Widespread introduction of [[flat screen television]]s in the late 1990s and early 2000s led to such availability.
# Thinner solar cells. High silicon prices in 2004–2008 encouraged silicon solar cell manufacturers to reduce silicon consumption by making them thinner; whereby 2008, according to Jef Poortmans, director of [[IMEC]]'s organic and solar department, cells used {{convert|8|-|9|g|oz}} of silicon per watt of power generation, with typical wafer thicknesses in the neighborhood of 200 [[microns]].

During the 1990s, [[polysilicon]] ("poly") cells became increasingly popular. These cells offer less efficiency than their mono-silicon ("mono") counterparts, but are grown in large vats that reduce cost. By the mid-2000s, poly was dominant in the low-cost panel market, but more recently the mono-silicon cells have returned to widespread use due to the efficiency gains.

[[Crystalline silicon]] panels dominate worldwide markets and are mostly manufactured in China and Taiwan. By late 2011, a drop in European demand dropped prices for crystalline solar modules to about $1.09<ref name="247wallst.com">[http://247wallst.com/2011/10/06/solar-stocks-does-the-punishment-fit-the-crime-fslr-spwra-stp-jaso-tsl-ldk-tan/ Solar Stocks: Does the Punishment Fit the Crime?]. 24/7 Wall St. (6 October 2011). Retrieved 3 January 2012.</ref> per watt down sharply from 2010. Prices continued to fall in 2012, reaching $0.62/watt by 4Q2012.<ref>{{cite web |last=Parkinson|first=Giles |title=Plunging Cost of Solar PV (Graphs) |publisher=Clean Technica |date=7 March 2013 |url=https://cleantechnica.com/2013/03/07/plunging-cost-of-solar-pv-graphs/ |access-date=18 May 2013}}</ref>

It was anticipated that electricity from PV will be competitive with wholesale electricity costs all across Europe and the energy payback time of crystalline silicon modules can be reduced to below 0.5 years by 2020.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mann |first1=Sander A. |last2=de Wild-Scholten |first2=Mariska J. |last3=Fthenakis |first3=Vasilis M. |last4=van Sark |first4=Wilfried G.J.H.M. |last5=Sinke |first5=Wim C. |date=1 November 2014 |title=The energy payback time of advanced crystalline silicon PV modules in 2020: a prospective study |journal=Progress in Photovoltaics: Research and Applications |volume=22 |issue=11 |pages=1180–1194 |doi=10.1002/pip.2363 |issn=1099-159X |s2cid=97151576 |hdl-access=free |hdl=1874/306424}}</ref>

Falling costs are considered one of the biggest factors in the rapid growth of [[renewable energy]], of 2016, solar PV is growing fastest in Asia, with China and Japan currently accounting for half of [[Growth of photovoltaics|worldwide deployment]].<ref name="iea-pvps-snapshot-1992-2014">
{{cite web |date=30 March 2015 |title=Snapshot of Global PV 1992–2014 |url=https://www.iea-pvps.org/fileadmin/dam/public/report/technical/PVPS_report_-_A_Snapshot_of_Global_PV_-_1992-2014.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150407023056/http://www.iea-pvps.org/index.php?id=92&eID=dam_frontend_push&docID=2430 |archive-date=7 April 2015 |publisher=International Energy Agency – Photovoltaic Power Systems Programme}}
</ref> Costs of solar photovoltaic electricity fell by ~85% between 2010 (when solar and wind made up 1.7% of global electricity generation) and 2021 (where they made up 8.7%).<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jaeger |first1=Joel |title=Explaining the Exponential Growth of Renewable Energy |url=https://www.wri.org/insights/growth-renewable-energy-sector-explained |access-date=8 November 2021 |language=en |date=20 September 2021}}</ref> Global installed PV capacity reached at least 301 gigawatts in 2016, and grew to supply 1.3% of global power by 2016.<ref>{{cite web |title=Solar energy – Renewable energy – Statistical Review of World Energy – Energy economics – BP |url=http://www.bp.com/en/global/corporate/energy-economics/statistical-review-of-world-energy/renewable-energy/solar-energy.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180323025347/https://www.bp.com/en/global/corporate/energy-economics/statistical-review-of-world-energy/renewable-energy/solar-energy.html |archive-date=23 March 2018 |access-date=2 September 2017 |website=bp.com}}</ref> In 2019 solar cells accounted for ~3% of the world's electricity generation at 720 Tw-hr.<ref name="techrev">{{cite web |title=Solar panels are a pain to recycle. These companies are trying to fix that. |website=MIT Technology Review |language=en |url=https://www.technologyreview.com/2021/08/19/1032215/solar-panels-recycling/ |access-date=8 November 2021}}</ref>

=== Subsidies and grid parity ===
Solar-specific [[feed-in tariff]]s vary by and within country countries. Such tariffs can encourage the development of solar power projects and to achieve grid parity. [[Grid parity]], the point at which photovoltaic electricity is equal to or cheaper than [[Mains electricity|grid power]] without subsidies, is expected to be first achieved in areas with abundant sun and high electricity costs such as in [[California]] and [[Japan]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bp.com/genericarticle.do?categoryId=9013609&contentId=7005395 |title=BP Global – Reports and publications – Going for grid parity |access-date=4 August 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110608081013/http://www.bp.com/genericarticle.do?categoryId=9013609&contentId=7005395 |archive-date=8 June 2011 }}. Bp.com. Retrieved 19 January 2011.</ref> In 2007 BP claimed grid parity for [[Hawaii]] and other islands that otherwise use [[diesel fuel]] to produce electricity. [[George W. Bush]] set 2015 as the date for grid parity in the US.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20131127115307/http://www.bp.com/liveassets/bp_internet/globalbp/globalbp_uk_english/reports_and_publications/frontiers/STAGING/local_assets/pdf/bpf19_14-18_solar.pdf BP Global – Reports and publications – Gaining on the grid]. Bp.com. August 2007.</ref><ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20131029191313/http://www.bp.com/popupimage.do?img_path=liveassets%2Fbp_internet%2Fglobalbp%2Fglobalbp_uk_english%2Freports_and_publications%2Ffrontiers%2FSTAGING%2Flocal_assets%2Fimages%2Ffr19solar_parity570x417.jpg%20&alt_tag=Graphic%20about%20grid%20parity%2C%20when%20the%20cost%20of%20solar%20energy%20equals%20that%20of%20grid%20electricity The Path to Grid Parity]. bp.com</ref> The Photovoltaic Association reported in 2012 that Australia had reached grid parity (ignoring feed in tariffs).<ref>Peacock, Matt (20 June 2012) [https://www.abc.net.au/news/2011-09-07/solar-industry-celebrates-grid-parity/2875592 Solar industry celebrates grid parity], ABC News.</ref>

The price of solar panels fell steadily for 40 years, interrupted in 2004 when high subsidies in Germany drastically increased demand there and greatly increased the price of purified silicon (which is used in computer chips as well as solar panels). The [[Great Recession]] and the onset of Chinese manufacturing caused prices to resume their decline. In the four years after January 2008 prices for solar modules in Germany dropped from €3 to €1 per peak watt. During that same time production capacity surged with an annual growth of more than 50%. China increased solar panel production market share from 8% in 2008 to over 55% in the last quarter of 2010.<ref>Baldwin, Sam (20 April 2011) [https://web.archive.org/web/20130512121859/http://energy.colostate.edu/docs/BaldwinTalkColoradoStateUniversity2011-04-20final.pdf Energy Efficiency & Renewable Energy: Challenges and Opportunities]. Clean Energy SuperCluster Expo Colorado State University. U.S. Department of Energy.</ref> In December 2012 the price of Chinese solar panels had dropped to $0.60/Wp (crystalline modules).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.enfsolar.com/news/Small-Chinese-Solar-Manufacturers-Decimated-in-2012 |title=Small Chinese Solar Manufacturers Decimated in 2012 |website=ENF Solar Trade Platform and Directory of Solar Companies |date=8 January 2013 |publisher=ENF Ltd. |access-date=1 June 2013 |archive-date=30 December 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171230030211/https://www.enfsolar.com/news/Small-Chinese-Solar-Manufacturers-Decimated-in-2012 }}</ref> (The abbreviation Wp stands for watt peak capacity, or the maximum capacity under optimal conditions.<ref>{{cite web|title=What is a solar panel and how does it work? |website=Energuide.be |publisher=Sibelga |url=https://www.energuide.be/en/questions-answers/what-is-a-solar-panel-and-how-does-it-work/136/ |access-date=2017-01-03}}</ref>)

As of the end of 2016, it was reported that [[Spot contract|spot prices]] for assembled solar ''panels'' (not cells) had fallen to a record-low of US$0.36/Wp. The second largest supplier, [[Canadian Solar]] Inc., had reported costs of US$0.37/Wp in the third quarter of 2016, having dropped $0.02 from the previous quarter, and hence was probably still at least breaking even. Many producers expected costs would drop to the vicinity of $0.30 by the end of 2017.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Martin|first1=Chris |date=30 December 2016|title=Solar Panels Now So Cheap Manufacturers Probably Selling at Loss|website=Bloomberg View |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2016-12-30/solar-panels-now-so-cheap-manufacturers-probably-selling-at-loss| publisher=Bloomberg LP|access-date=3 January 2017}}</ref> It was also reported that new solar installations were cheaper than coal-based thermal power plants in some regions of the world, and this was expected to be the case in most of the world within a decade.<ref>{{cite news| last1=Shankleman|first1=Jessica|last2=Martin|first2=Chris| title=Solar Could Beat Coal to Become the Cheapest Power on Earth| url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2017-01-03/for-cheapest-power-on-earth-look-skyward-as-coal-falls-to-solar| access-date=3 January 2017| work=Bloomberg View| publisher=Bloomberg LP| date=3 January 2017}}</ref>

== Theory ==
[[File:Solartce3.gif|thumb|Schematic of charge collection by solar cells. Light transmits through transparent conducting electrode creating electron hole pairs, which are collected by both the electrodes.<ref name=kumar2017 />]]
[[File:Silicon Solar cell structure and mechanism.svg|thumb|Working [[Theory of solar cells|mechanism]] of a solar cell]]
{{Main|Theory of solar cells}}

A solar cell is made of [[List of semiconductor materials|semiconducting materials]], such as [[silicon]], that have been fabricated into a [[p–n junction]]. Such junctions are made by [[Doping (semiconductor)|doping]] one side of the device p-type and the other n-type, for example in the case of silicon by introducing small concentrations of [[boron]] or [[phosphorus]] respectively.

Solar cells use "doping" of the silicon substrate to lower the activation energy thereby making the cell more efficient in converting photons to retrievable electrons.<ref>{{cite web |date=April 2000 |title=How Solar Cells Work |url=http://science.howstuffworks.com/environmental/energy/solar-cell2.htm |access-date=2015-12-09 |website=HowStuffWorks}}</ref> Doping chemicals such as [[boron]] (p-type) are applied into the semiconductor crystal in order to create donor and acceptor energy levels substantially closer to the valence and conductor bands.<ref>{{cite web |title=Bonding in Metals and Semiconductors |url=https://2012books.lardbucket.org/books/principles-of-general-chemistry-v1.0/s16-06-bonding-in-metals-and-semicond.html |access-date=2015-12-09 |website=2012books.lardbucket.org}}</ref> In doing so, the addition of boron impurity allows the activation energy to decrease twenty-fold from 1.12&nbsp;eV to 0.05&nbsp;eV. Since the potential difference (E<sub>B</sub>) is so low, the boron is able to thermally ionize at room temperatures. This allows for free energy carriers in the conduction and valence bands thereby allowing greater conversion of photons to electrons.

In operation, [[photon]]s in [[sunlight]] hit the solar cell and are absorbed by the semiconductor. When the photons are absorbed, [[electrons]] are [[Excited state|excited]] from the [[Valence and conduction bands|valence band to the conduction band]] (or from [[HOMO and LUMO|occupied to unoccupied molecular orbitals]] in the case of an [[organic solar cell]]), producing [[Semiconductor#Charge carriers (electrons and holes)|electron-hole pairs]]. If the electron-hole pairs are created near the junction between p-type and n-type materials the local electric field sweeps them apart to opposite electrodes, producing an excess of electrons on one side and an excess of holes on the other. When the solar cell is unconnected (or the external [[electrical load]] is very high) the electrons and holes will ultimately restore equilibrium by [[Diffusion current|diffusing]] back across the junction against the field and [[Relaxation (physics)|recombine]] with each other giving off heat, but if the load is small enough then it is easier for equilibrium to be restored by the excess electrons going around the external circuit, doing useful work along the way.

The most commonly known solar cell is configured as a large-area [[p–n junction]] made from silicon. Other possible solar cell types are organic solar cells, dye sensitized solar cells, perovskite solar cells, quantum dot solar cells, etc. The illuminated side of a solar cell generally has a [[transparent conducting film]] for allowing light to enter into the active material and to collect the generated charge carriers. Typically, films with high transmittance and high electrical conductance such as [[indium tin oxide]], conducting polymers, or conducting nanowire networks are used for the purpose.<ref name="kumar2017">{{Cite journal|last=Kumar|first=Ankush|date=3 January 2017|title=Predicting efficiency of solar cells based on transparent conducting electrodes|journal=Journal of Applied Physics|volume=121|issue=1|article-number=014502|doi=10.1063/1.4973117|issn=0021-8979|bibcode=2017JAP...121a4502K}}</ref>

== Efficiency ==
[[File:ShockleyQueisserFullCurve.svg|thumb|The [[Shockley–Queisser limit]] for the theoretical maximum efficiency of a solar cell. Semiconductors with [[band gap]] between 1 and 1.5[[Electronvolt|eV]] (827 nm to 1240 nm; near-infrared) have the greatest potential to form an efficient single-junction cell. (The efficiency "limit" shown here can be exceeded by [[multijunction solar cell]]s.)]]
{{Main|Solar cell efficiency}}

Solar cell efficiency may be broken down into reflectance efficiency, thermodynamic efficiency, charge carrier separation efficiency and conductive efficiency. The overall efficiency is the product of these individual metrics.

The [[Energy conversion efficiency|power conversion efficiency]] of a solar cell is a parameter which is defined by the fraction of incident power converted into electricity.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.pveducation.org/pvcdrom/solar-cell-efficiency|title=Solar Cell Efficiency {{!}} PVEducation|website=www.pveducation.org|access-date=31 January 2018|archive-date=31 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180131050352/http://pveducation.org/pvcdrom/solar-cell-efficiency}}</ref>

A solar cell has a voltage dependent efficiency curve, temperature coefficients, and allowable shadow angles.

Due to the difficulty in measuring these parameters directly, other parameters are substituted: thermodynamic efficiency, [[Quantum efficiency of a solar cell|quantum efficiency]], [[Quantum efficiency|integrated quantum efficiency]], [[open-circuit voltage|V<sub>OC</sub>]] ratio, and fill factor. Reflectance losses are a portion of quantum efficiency under "[[Quantum efficiency#Types of quantum efficiency|external quantum efficiency]]". Recombination losses make up another portion of quantum efficiency, V<sub>OC</sub> ratio, and fill factor. Resistive losses are predominantly categorized under fill factor, but also make up minor portions of quantum efficiency, V<sub>OC</sub> ratio.

The ''[[fill factor (solar cell)|fill factor]]'' is the ratio of the actual maximum obtainable [[power (physics)|power]] to the product of the [[open-circuit voltage]] and [[short-circuit current]]. This is a key parameter in evaluating performance. In 2009, typical commercial solar cells had a fill factor > 0.70. Grade B cells were usually between 0.4 and 0.7.<ref>{{cite web| title=T.Bazouni: What is the Fill Factor of a Solar Panel| url=http://www.solarfreaks.com/what-is-the-fill-factor-of-a-solar-panel-t138.html| access-date=17 February 2009| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090415162123/http://www.solarfreaks.com/what-is-the-fill-factor-of-a-solar-panel-t138.html| archive-date=15 April 2009}}</ref> Cells with a high fill factor have a low [[equivalent series resistance]] and a high [[Shunt (electrical)|equivalent shunt resistance]], so less of the current produced by the cell is dissipated in internal losses.

Single p–n junction crystalline silicon devices are now approaching the theoretical limiting power efficiency of 33.16%,<ref>{{Cite journal|title = Tabulated Values of the Shockley-Queisser Limit for Single Junction Solar Cells |journal = Solar Energy|date = 8 February 2016|pages = 139–147|volume = 130|doi = 10.1016/j.solener.2016.02.015 |first = Sven|last = Rühle|bibcode = 2016SoEn..130..139R}}</ref> noted as the [[Shockley–Queisser limit]] in 1961. In the extreme, with an infinite number of layers, the corresponding limit is 86% using concentrated sunlight.<ref name=Vos80>{{Cite journal | doi = 10.1088/0022-3727/13/5/018| title = Detailed balance limit of the efficiency of tandem solar cells| journal = Journal of Physics D: Applied Physics| volume = 13| issue = 5| page = 839| year = 1980| last1 = Vos | first1 = A. D. | bibcode = 1980JPhD...13..839D| s2cid = 250782402}}</ref>

[[File:Best Research-Cell Efficiencies.svg|thumb|upright=1.5|Reported timeline of research solar cell energy conversion efficiencies ([[National Renewable Energy Laboratory]])]]

In 2014, three companies broke the record of 25.6% for a silicon solar cell. Panasonic's was the most efficient. The company moved the front contacts to the rear of the panel, eliminating shaded areas. In addition they applied thin silicon films to the (high quality silicon) wafer's front and back to eliminate defects at or near the wafer surface.<ref>Bullis, Kevin (13 June 2014) [http://www.technologyreview.com/news/528351/record-breaking-solar-cell-points-the-way-to-cheaper-power Record-Breaking Solar Cell Points the Way to Cheaper Power]. ''MIT Technology Review''</ref>

In 2015, a 4-junction {{chem2|GaInP}}/{{chem2|GaAs}}//{{chem2|GaInAsP}}/{{chem2|GaInAs}} solar cell achieved a new laboratory record efficiency of 46.1% (concentration ratio of sunlight = 312) in a French-German collaboration between the [[Fraunhofer Institute for Solar Energy Systems ISE|Fraunhofer Institute for Solar Energy Systems (Fraunhofer ISE)]], CEA-LETI and SOITEC.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dimroth |first1=Frank |last2=Tibbits |first2=Thomas N.D. |last3=Niemeyer |first3=Markus |last4=Predan |first4=Felix |last5=Beutel |first5=Paul |last6=Karcher |first6=Christian |last7=Oliva |first7=Eduard |last8=Siefer |first8=Gerald |last9=Lackner |display-authors=etal |first9=David |year=2016 |title=Four-Junction Wafer Bonded Concentrator Solar Cells |journal=IEEE Journal of Photovoltaics |volume=6 |issue=1 |pages=343–349 |s2cid=47576267 |url=https://publica.fraunhofer.de/bitstreams/aabc6d11-d9f6-4f1e-8bc6-569ce93d630d/download |doi=10.1109/jphotov.2015.2501729|url-access= }}</ref>

In September 2015, [[Fraunhofer Institute for Solar Energy Systems ISE|Fraunhofer ISE]] announced the achievement of an efficiency above 20% for [[epitaxial wafer]] cells. The work on optimizing the atmospheric-pressure [[chemical vapor deposition]] (APCVD) in-line production chain was done in collaboration with NexWafe GmbH, a company spun off from Fraunhofer ISE to commercialize production.<ref name=NexWafe>{{cite web| title=20% Efficient Solar Cell on EpiWafer| url=https://www.ise.fraunhofer.de/en/press-and-media/press-releases/press-releases-2015/nexwafe| first1=Stefan| last1=Janz| first2=Stefan| last2=Reber| publisher=[[Fraunhofer Institute for Applied Solid State Physics|Fraunhofer ISE]] |date=14 September 2015| access-date=15 October 2015}}</ref><ref name="DrießenAmiri2016">{{cite journal |last1=Drießen |first1=Marion |last2=Amiri |first2=Diana |last3=Milenkovic |first3=Nena |last4=Steinhauser |first4=Bernd |last5=Lindekugel |first5=Stefan |last6=Benick |first6=Jan |last7=Reber |first7=Stefan |last8=Janz |first8=Stefan |title=Solar Cells with 20% Efficiency and Lifetime Evaluation of Epitaxial Wafers |journal=Energy Procedia |volume=92 |year=2016 |pages=785–790 |issn=1876-6102 |doi=10.1016/j.egypro.2016.07.069| doi-access=free|bibcode=2016EnPro..92..785D |url=https://publica.fraunhofer.de/bitstreams/85e223be-f1eb-46a7-9f89-e33164a7ab89/download }}</ref>

For triple-junction thin-film solar cells, the world record is 13.6%, set in June 2015.<ref>{{cite web|title = Solar cell sets world record with a stabilized efficiency of 13.6%|url = http://phys.org/news/2015-06-solar-cell-world-stabilized-efficiency.html |date=4 June 2015 |author=Zyg, Lisa |work=Phys.org}}</ref>

In 2016, researchers at [[Fraunhofer Institute for Solar Energy Systems ISE|Fraunhofer ISE]] announced a {{chem2|GaInP}}/{{chem2|GaAs}}/{{chem2|Si}} triple-junction solar cell with two terminals reaching 30.2% efficiency without concentration.<ref>[https://nationalsolarclean.com/ 30.2% Efficiency – New Record for Silicon-based Multi-junction Solar Cell]. (9 November 2016). Retrieved 15 November 2016.</ref>

In 2017, a team of researchers at [[National Renewable Energy Laboratory]] (NREL), [[École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne|EPFL]] and [[Swiss Center for Electronics and Microtechnology|CSEM]] ([[Switzerland]]) reported record one-sun efficiencies of 32.8% for dual-junction {{chem2|GaInP}}/{{chem2|GaAs}} solar cell devices. In addition, the dual-junction device was mechanically stacked with a Si solar cell, to achieve a record one-sun efficiency of 35.9% for triple-junction solar cells.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Essig |first1=Stephanie |last2=Allebé |first2=Christophe |last3=Remo |first3=Timothy |last4=Geisz |first4=John F. |last5=Steiner |first5=Myles A. |last6=Horowitz |first6=Kelsey |last7=Barraud |first7=Loris |last8=Ward |first8=J. Scott |last9=Schnabel |first9=Manuel |date=September 2017 |title=Raising the one-sun conversion efficiency of III–V/Si solar cells to 32.8% for two junctions and 35.9% for three junctions |journal=Nature Energy |volume=2 |issue=9 |page=17144 |doi=10.1038/nenergy.2017.144 |issn=2058-7546 |bibcode=2017NatEn...217144E |s2cid=115327057 |url=https://infoscience.epfl.ch/record/232878/files/paper_882.pdf }}</ref>
