 |url-status=live
 }}</ref>{{rp|24,25}}]]

Solar cells are typically named after the [[semiconducting material]] of which they are composed. These [[List of semiconductor materials|materials]] have varying characteristics to absorb optimal available [[sunlight]] spectrum. Some cells are designed to handle sunlight that reaches the Earth's surface, while others are optimized for [[Solar panels on spacecraft|use in space]]. Solar cells can be made of a single layer of light-absorbing material ([[p–n junction|single-junction]]) or use multiple physical configurations ([[multi-junction]]s) to take advantage of various absorption and charge separation mechanisms.

Solar cells can be classified into first, second and third generation:

# First generation cells—also called conventional, traditional or [[Wafer (electronics)|wafer]]-based cells—are made of [[crystalline silicon]], the commercially predominant PV technology, that includes materials such as [[polysilicon]] and [[monocrystalline silicon]].
# Second generation cells are [[thin film solar cells]], that include [[amorphous silicon]], [[Cadmium telluride photovoltaics|CdTe]] and [[Copper indium gallium selenide solar cell|CIGS]] cells and are commercially significant in utility-scale [[photovoltaic power station]]s, [[building integrated photovoltaics]] or in small [[stand-alone power system]]. 
# [[third-generation photovoltaic cell|Third generation of solar cells]] includes a number of thin-film technologies often described as emerging photovoltaics—most of them have not yet been commercially applied and are still in the research or development phase. Many use organic materials, often [[organometallic]] compounds as well as inorganic substances. Despite the fact that their efficiencies had been low and the stability of the absorber material was often too short for commercial applications, there is research into these technologies as they promise to achieve the goal of producing low-cost, high-efficiency solar cells.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Marques Lameirinhas |first1=Ricardo A. |last2=Torres |first2=João Paulo N. |last3=de Melo Cunha |first3=João P. |date=January 2022 |title=A Photovoltaic Technology Review: History, Fundamentals and Applications |journal=Energies |language=en |volume=15 |issue=5 |page=1823 |doi=10.3390/en15051823 |issn=1996-1073|doi-access=free |bibcode=2022Energ..15.1823M }}</ref>

As of 2016, the most popular and efficient solar cells were those made from thin wafers of silicon which are also the oldest solar cell technology.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ranabhat |first1=Kiran |last2=Patrikeev |first2=Leev |last3=Antal'evna-Revina |first3=Aleksandra |last4=Andrianov |first4=Kirill |last5=Lapshinsky |first5=Valerii |last6=Sofronova |first6=Elena |date=2016 |title=An introduction to solar cell technology |journal=Istrazivanja i Projektovanja za Privredu |language=en |volume=14 |issue=4 |pages=481–491 |doi=10.5937/jaes14-10879 |issn=1451-4117 |s2cid=114726770 |doi-access=free}}</ref>

=== Crystalline silicon ===
{{Main|Crystalline silicon}}
By far, the most prevalent bulk material for solar cells is [[crystalline silicon]] (c-Si), also known as "solar grade silicon". Bulk silicon is separated into multiple categories according to crystallinity and crystal size in the resulting [[ingot]], [[ribbon]] or [[Wafer (electronics)|wafer]]. These cells are entirely based around the concept of a [[p–n junction]]. Solar cells made of c-Si are made from [[wafer (electronics)|wafers]] between 160 and 240&nbsp;micrometers thick.

==== Monocrystalline silicon ====
{{Main|Monocrystalline silicon}}
[[File:Sono Sion Front Back.jpg|thumb|The roof, bonnet and large parts of the outer shell of the [[Sion (electric car)|Sion]] are equipped with highly efficient monocrystalline silicon cells.]]

[[Monocrystalline silicon]] (mono-Si) solar cells feature a single-crystal composition that enables electrons to move more freely than in a multi-crystal configuration. Consequently, monocrystalline solar panels deliver a higher efficiency than their multicrystalline counterparts.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.targray.com/solar/pv-modules/monocrystalline |title=Monocrystalline Solar Modules |access-date=2020-08-27}}</ref> The corners of the cells look clipped, like an octagon, because the wafer material is cut from cylindrical ingots, that are typically grown by the [[Czochralski process]]. Solar panels using mono-Si cells display a distinctive pattern of small white diamonds.

==== Epitaxial silicon development ====
[[Epitaxial wafer]]s of crystalline silicon can be grown on a monocrystalline silicon "seed" wafer by [[chemical vapor deposition]] (CVD), and then detached as self-supporting wafers of some standard thickness (e.g., 250&nbsp;μm) that can be manipulated by hand, and directly substituted for wafer cells cut from monocrystalline silicon ingots. Solar cells made with this "[[Saw#Terminology|kerf]]less" technique can have efficiencies approaching those of wafer-cut cells, but at appreciably lower cost if the CVD can be done at [[atmospheric pressure]] in a high-throughput inline process.<ref name=NexWafe/><ref name="DrießenAmiri2016"/> The surface of epitaxial wafers may be textured to enhance light absorption.<ref name=pyramid>{{cite journal| doi=10.1021/acs.nanolett.6b01240 | pmid=27525513 | title=Ultrathin Epitaxial Silicon Solar Cells with Inverted Nanopyramid Arrays for Efficient Light Trapping | first1= Alexandre| last1= Gaucher| first2= Andrea| last2= Cattoni| first3= Christophe| last3= Dupuis| first4= Wanghua| last4= Chen| first5= Romain| last5= Cariou|last6 = Foldyna| first6=Martin | last7=Lalouat | first7=Loı̈c | last8=Drouard | first8=Emmanuel | last9=Seassal | first9=Christian | last10=Roca i Cabarrocas | first10=Pere | last11=Collin | first11=Stéphane | journal= Nano Letters| year= 2016| volume= 16| issue=9 | pages= 5358–64| bibcode=2016NanoL..16.5358G | s2cid=206734456 | url=https://hal-polytechnique.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-01401081/file/Gaucher2016.pdf }}</ref><ref name="ChenCariou2016">{{cite journal | last1=Chen | first1=Wanghua | last2=Cariou | first2=Romain | last3=Foldyna | first3=Martin | last4=Depauw | first4=Valerie | last5=Trompoukis | first5=Christos | last6=Drouard | first6=Emmanuel | last7=Lalouat | first7=Loic | last8=Harouri | first8=Abdelmounaim | last9=Liu | first9=Jia | last10=Fave | first10=Alain | last11=Orobtchouk | first11=Régis | last12=Mandorlo | first12=Fabien | last13=Seassal | first13=Christian | last14=Massiot | first14=Inès | last15=Dmitriev | first15=Alexandre | last16=Lee | first16=Ki-Dong | last17=Cabarrocas | first17=Pere Roca i | title=Nanophotonics-based low-temperature PECVD epitaxial crystalline silicon solar cells | journal=Journal of Physics D: Applied Physics | volume=49 | issue=12 | year=2016 | article-number=125603 | issn=0022-3727 | doi=10.1088/0022-3727/49/12/125603| bibcode=2016JPhD...49l5603C | s2cid=125317340 }}</ref>

In June 2015, it was reported that [[heterojunction]] solar cells grown epitaxially on n-type monocrystalline silicon wafers had reached an efficiency of 22.5% over a total cell area of 243.4&nbsp;cm<math>^2</math>.<ref name="KobayashiWatabe2015">{{cite journal|last1=Kobayashi|first1=Eiji|last2=Watabe|first2=Yoshimi|last3=Hao|first3=Ruiying|last4=Ravi|first4=T. S.|title=High efficiency heterojunction solar cells on n-type kerfless mono crystalline silicon wafers by epitaxial growth|journal=Applied Physics Letters|volume=106|issue=22|year=2015|page=223504|issn=0003-6951|doi=10.1063/1.4922196|bibcode=2015ApPhL.106v3504K}}</ref> Heterojunction solar cells reached mass-production some time before 2022.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Wang |first=Vera |date=13 February 2023 |title=N-type capacity ramps up: Over 900 GW planned by 60 manufacturers |work=Solarbe Global |url=https://www.solarbeglobal.com/n-type-capacity-ramps-up-over-900-gw-announced-by-60-manufacturers/}}</ref> It had also overtaken aluminum-back surface field (Al-BSF) panels in market share. However, it is not expected to outgrow conventional TOPCon (Tunnel Oxide Passivated Contact) due to similar theoretical efficiency ceilings and higher production costs.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Barrows |first1= Alex |last2=Morgan |first2=Molly|date=2025-08-01 |title=Non-stop solar innovation despite module oversupply |url=https://www.pv-magazine.com/2025/08/01/non-stop-solar-innovation-despite-module-oversupply/ |access-date=2025-08-05 |website=pv magazine International |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Polycrystalline silicon ====
{{Main|Polycrystalline silicon}}
[[Polycrystalline silicon]], or multicrystalline silicon (multi-Si) cells are made from cast square ingots—large blocks of molten silicon carefully cooled and solidified. They consist of small crystals giving the material its typical [[Crystallite|metal flake effect]]. Polysilicon cells are the most common type used in photovoltaics and are less expensive, but also less efficient, than those made from monocrystalline silicon.

==== Ribbon silicon ====
[[Ribbon silicon]] is a type of polycrystalline silicon—it is formed by drawing flat thin films from [[molten]] silicon and results in a polycrystalline structure. These cells are cheaper to make than multi-Si, due to a great reduction in silicon waste, as this approach does not require [[saw]]ing from [[ingot]]s.<ref>{{cite book|chapter-url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/4078712|chapter=String ribbon silicon solar cells with 17.8% efficiency|author=Kim, D.S.|title=Proceedings of 3rd World Conference on Photovoltaic Energy Conversion, 2003|date=18 May 2003|pages= 1293–1296|volume=2|isbn=978-4-9901816-0-4
|display-authors=etal }}</ref> However, they are also less efficient.

==== Mono-like-multi silicon (MLM) ====
This form was developed in the 2000s and introduced commercially around 2009. Also called cast-mono, this design uses polycrystalline casting chambers with small "seeds" of mono material. The result is a bulk mono-like material that is polycrystalline around the outsides. When sliced for processing, the inner sections are high-efficiency mono-like cells (but square instead of "clipped"), while the outer edges are sold as conventional poly. This production method results in mono-like cells at poly-like prices.<ref>Wayne McMillan, [http://btimaging.com/Downloads/bt_PV_eng-Advertorial3_Final.pdf "The Cast Mono Dilemma"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131105134839/http://www.btimaging.com/downloads/bt_pv_eng-advertorial3_final.pdf |date=5 November 2013 }}, BT Imaging</ref>

=== Thin film ===
{{Main|Thin-film solar cell}}
Thin-film technologies reduce the amount of active material in a cell. Most designs sandwich active material between two panes of glass. Since silicon solar panels only use one pane of glass, thin film panels are approximately twice as heavy as crystalline silicon panels, although they have a smaller ecological impact (determined from [[life cycle analysis]]).<ref>{{Cite book| doi = 10.1115/SED2002-1051| chapter = Net Energy Analysis for Sustainable Energy Production from Silicon Based Solar Cells| title = Solar Energy| page = 181| year = 2002| last1 = Pearce| first1 = J.| last2 = Lau| first2 = A.| isbn = 978-0-7918-1689-9| chapter-url = http://www.cede.psu.edu/users/alau/ases02_net_energy_pv.pdf}}{{dead link|date=May 2020|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><!-- The majority of film panels have 2–3 percentage points lower conversion efficiencies than crystalline silicon.<ref>Datasheets of the market leaders: ''First Solar'' for thin film, ''Suntech'' and ''SunPower'' for crystalline silicon</ref> [[Cadmium telluride]] (CdTe), [[copper indium gallium selenide]] (CIGS) and [[amorphous silicon]] (a-Si) are three thin-film technologies often used for outdoor applications. As of December 2013, CdTe cost per installed watt was $0.59 as reported by First Solar. CIGS technology laboratory demonstrations reached 20.4% conversion efficiency as of December 2013. The lab efficiency of GaAs thin film technology topped 28%.{{citation needed|date=June 2014}} The [[quantum efficiency]] of thin film solar cells is also lower due to reduced number of collected charge carriers per incident photon. Most recently, CZTS solar cell emerge as the less-toxic thin film solar cell technology, which achieved ~12% efficiency. --><ref>{{Cite journal|title = Thin Film Solar Cells: Research in an Industrial Perspective|journal = Ambio|date = March 2012|issn = 0044-7447|pmc = 3357764|pmid = 22434436|pages = 112–118|volume = 41|issue = 2|doi = 10.1007/s13280-012-0265-6|first = Marika|last = Edoff| bibcode=2012Ambio..41S.112E }}</ref>

==== Cadmium telluride ====
{{Main|Cadmium telluride photovoltaics}}
Cadmium telluride is the only thin film material so far to rival crystalline silicon in cost/watt. However cadmium is highly toxic and [[tellurium]] ([[anion]]: "telluride") supplies are limited. The [[cadmium]] present in the cells would be toxic if released. However, release is impossible during normal operation of the cells and is unlikely during fires in residential roofs.<ref name="Fthenakis 303–334">{{Cite journal
| last = Fthenakis
| first = Vasilis M.
| title = Life cycle impact analysis of cadmium in CdTe PV production
| journal = Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews
| volume = 8
| issue = 4
| pages = 303–334
| year= 2004
| url = http://www.clca.columbia.edu/papers/Life_Cycle_Impact_Analysis_Cadmium_CdTe_Photovoltaic_production.pdf
| doi = 10.1016/j.rser.2003.12.001| bibcode = 2004RSERv...8..303F
}}</ref> A square meter of CdTe contains approximately the same amount of Cd as a single C cell [[nickel-cadmium battery]], in a more stable and less soluble form.<ref name="Fthenakis 303–334" />

==== Copper indium gallium selenide ====
{{Main|Copper indium gallium selenide solar cell}}
Copper indium gallium selenide (CIGS) is a [[Direct and indirect band gaps|direct band gap]] material. It has the highest efficiency (~20%) among all commercially significant thin film materials (see [[Copper indium gallium selenide solar cell|CIGS solar cell]]). Traditional methods of fabrication involve vacuum processes including co-evaporation and sputtering. Recent developments at [[IBM]] and [[Nanosolar]] attempt to lower the cost by using non-vacuum solution processes.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20080620043314/http://www-03.ibm.com/press/us/en/pressrelease/24464.wss "IBM and Tokyo Ohka Kogyo Turn Up Watts on Solar Energy Production"], IBM</ref>

==== Silicon thin film ====
[[Silicon thin-film cell]]s are mainly deposited by [[chemical vapor deposition]] (typically plasma-enhanced, PE-CVD) from [[silane]] gas and [[hydrogen]] gas. Depending on the deposition parameters, this can yield [[amorphous silicon]] (a-Si or a-Si:H), [[protocrystalline]] silicon or [[nanocrystalline silicon]] (nc-Si or nc-Si:H), also called microcrystalline silicon.<ref>{{Cite journal | doi = 10.1016/S0927-0248(02)00436-1| title = Evolution of microstructure and phase in amorphous, protocrystalline, and microcrystalline silicon studied by real time spectroscopic ellipsometry| journal = Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells| volume = 78| issue = 1–4| page = 143| year = 2003| last1 = Collins | first1 = R. W. | last2 = Ferlauto | first2 = A. S. | last3 = Ferreira | first3 = G. M. | last4 = Chen | first4 = C. | last5 = Koh | first5 = J. | last6 = Koval | first6 = R. J. | last7 = Lee | first7 = Y. | last8 = Pearce | first8 = J. M. | last9 = Wronski | first9 = C. R.
| bibcode = 2003SEMSC..78..143C}}</ref>

Amorphous silicon is the most well-developed thin film technology to-date. An amorphous silicon (a-Si) solar cell is made of non-crystalline or microcrystalline silicon. Amorphous silicon has a higher bandgap (1.7&nbsp;eV) than crystalline silicon (c-Si) (1.1&nbsp;eV), which means it absorbs the visible part of the solar spectrum more strongly than the higher power density [[infrared]] portion of the spectrum. The production of a-Si thin film solar cells uses glass as a substrate and deposits a very thin layer of silicon by [[plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition]] (PECVD).

Protocrystalline silicon with a low volume fraction of nanocrystalline silicon is optimal for high open-circuit voltage.<ref>{{Cite journal| doi=10.1063/1.2714507| title=Optimization of open circuit voltage in amorphous silicon solar cells with mixed-phase (amorphous+nanocrystalline) p-type contacts of low nanocrystalline content| journal=Journal of Applied Physics| volume=101| issue=11| pages=114301–114301–7| year=2007| last1=Pearce| first1=J. M.| last2=Podraza| first2=N.| last3=Collins| first3=R. W.| last4=Al-Jassim| first4=M. M.| last5=Jones| first5=K. M.| last6=Deng| first6=J.| last7=Wronski| first7=C. R.| url=http://me.queensu.ca/people/pearce/publications/documents/t14.pdf| bibcode=2007JAP...101k4301P| archive-url=https://archive.wikiwix.com/cache/20090613201701/http://me.queensu.ca/people/pearce/publications/documents/t14.pdf| archive-date=13 June 2009}}</ref> Nc-Si has about the same bandgap as c-Si and nc-Si and a-Si can advantageously be combined in thin layers, creating a layered cell called a tandem cell. The top cell in a-Si absorbs the visible light and leaves the infrared part of the spectrum for the bottom cell in nc-Si.

==== Gallium arsenide thin film ====
The semiconductor material [[gallium arsenide]] (GaAs) is also used for single-crystalline thin film solar cells. Although GaAs cells are very expensive,<ref>{{Cite book |last=Green |first=Martin A. |title=Third Generation Photovoltaics: Advanced Solar Energy Conversion |publisher=Springer |year=2003 |pages=112–118}}</ref> they hold the world's record in efficiency for a [[Single-junction cell|single-junction]] solar cell at 28.8%.<ref>{{cite book |doi=10.1109/PVSC.2012.6317891 |chapter=The opto-electronic physics that broke the efficiency limit in solar cells |title=2012 38th IEEE Photovoltaic Specialists Conference |year=2012 |last1=Yablonovitch |first1=Eli |last2=Miller |first2=Owen D. |last3=Kurtz |first3=S. R. |author3-link=Sarah Kurtz |isbn=978-1-4673-0066-7 |at=001556 |s2cid=30141399}}</ref> Typically fabricated on crystalline silicon wafer<ref>{{Cite web |last= |title=Thin-Film Trick Makes Gallium Arsenide Devices Cheap |url=https://spectrum.ieee.org/thinfilm-trick-makes-gallium-arsenide-devices-cheap |access-date=2023-06-26 |website=IEEE Spectrum |language=en}}</ref> with a 41% [[Fill factor (solar cell)|fill factor]], by moving to porous silicon fill factor can be increased to 56% with potentially reduced cost. Using less active GaAs material by fabricating nanowires is another potential pathway to cost reduction.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gemini |first=Redaksjonen |date=2021-11-05 |title=New ultra-high material efficient, low-cost solar cells — using nanowires |url=https://norwegianscitechnews.com/2021/11/new-ultra-high-material-efficient-low-cost-solar-cells-using-nanowires/ |access-date=2023-06-26 |website=Norwegian SciTech News |language=en-US}}</ref> GaAs is more commonly used in [[multijunction photovoltaic cell]]s for [[concentrated photovoltaics]] (CPV, HCPV) and for [[solar panels on spacecraft]], as the industry favours efficiency over cost for [[space-based solar power]].

=== Multijunction cells ===
{{Main|Multi-junction solar cell}}
[[File:Dawn solar panel.jpg|thumb|[[Dawn (spacecraft)|Dawn]]'s 10&nbsp;[[Kilowatt|kW]] triple-junction gallium arsenide solar array at full extension]]

Multi-junction cells consist of multiple thin films, each essentially a solar cell grown on top of another, typically using [[metalorganic vapour phase epitaxy]]. Each layer has a different band gap energy to allow it to absorb [[electromagnetic radiation]] over a different portion of the spectrum. Multi-junction cells were originally developed for special applications such as [[satellite]]s and [[space exploration]], but are now used increasingly in terrestrial [[concentrator photovoltaics]] (CPV), an emerging technology that uses lenses and curved mirrors to concentrate sunlight onto small, highly efficient multi-junction solar cells. By concentrating sunlight up to a thousand times, ''High concentration photovoltaics (HCPV)'' has the potential to outcompete conventional solar PV in the future.<ref name=Fraunhofer-PR-2014>
{{cite web
 |title = Photovoltaics Report
 |url = http://www.ise.fraunhofer.de/en/downloads-englisch/pdf-files-englisch/photovoltaics-report-slides.pdf
 |publisher = Fraunhofer ISE
 |access-date = 31 August 2014
 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20140809192020/http://www.ise.fraunhofer.de/en/downloads-englisch/pdf-files-englisch/photovoltaics-report-slides.pdf
 |archive-date = 9 August 2014
 |date = 28 July 2014
 |url-status = live
 }}</ref>{{rp|21,26}}

Tandem solar cells based on monolithic, series connected, [[gallium indium phosphide]] ({{chem2|GaInP}}), [[gallium arsenide]] ({{chem2|GaAs}}), and [[germanium]] ({{chem2|Ge}}) p–n junctions, are increasing sales, despite cost pressures.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Oku|first=Takeo|author2=Kumada, Kazuma |author3=Suzuki, Atsushi |author4= Kikuchi, Kenji |title=Effects of germanium addition to copper phthalocyanine/fullerene-based solar cells|journal=Central European Journal of Engineering|date=June 2012|volume=2|issue=2|pages=248–252|doi=10.2478/s13531-011-0069-7|bibcode=2012CEJE....2..248O|s2cid=136518369|doi-access=free}}</ref> Between December 2006 and December 2007, the cost of 4N gallium metal rose from about $350 per kg to $680 per kg. Additionally, germanium metal prices have risen substantially to $1000–1200 per kg this year. Those materials include gallium (4N, 6N and 7N Ga), arsenic (4N, 6N and 7N) and germanium, pyrolytic boron nitride (pBN) crucibles for growing crystals, and boron oxide, these products are critical to the entire substrate manufacturing industry.<ref>Wikipedia contributors, Solar cell, Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 2025, online article.</ref>

A triple-junction cell, for example, may consist of the semiconductors: [[Gallium(III) arsenide|GaAs]], [[Germanium|Ge]], and [[Indium gallium phosphide|{{chem|GaInP|2}}]].<ref>[https://www.spectrolab.com/DataSheets/TerCel/tercell.pdf Triple-Junction Terrestrial Concentrator Solar Cells]. (PDF) Retrieved 3 January 2012.</ref> Triple-junction GaAs solar cells were used as the power source of the Dutch four-time [[World Solar Challenge]] winners [[Nuna 4|Nuna]] in 2003, 2005 and 2007 and by the Dutch solar cars [[Solutra|Solutra (2005)]], [[Twente One|Twente One (2007)]] and 21Revolution (2009).<ref>Wikipedia contributors, Solar cell, Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 2025, online article.
</ref> GaAs based multi-junction devices are the most efficient solar cells to date. On 15 October 2012, triple junction metamorphic cells reached a record high of 44%.<ref>Clarke, Chris (19 April 2011) [https://www.kcet.org/news/rewire/solar/photovoltaic-pv/san-jose-solar-company-breaks-efficiency-record-for-pv.html San Jose Solar Company Breaks Efficiency Record for PV]. Optics.org. Retrieved 19 January 2011.</ref> In 2022, researchers at Fraunhofer Institute for Solar Energy Systems ISE in Freiburg, Germany, demonstrated a record solar cell efficiency of 47.6% under 665-fold sunlight concentration with a four-junction concentrator solar cell.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-05-30 |title=Fraunhofer ISE Develops the World's Most Efficient Solar Cell with 47.6 Percent Efficiency - Fraunhofer ISE |url=https://www.ise.fraunhofer.de/en/press-media/press-releases/2022/fraunhofer-ise-develops-the-worlds-most-efficient-solar-cell-with-47-comma-6-percent-efficiency.html |access-date=2024-07-23 |website=Fraunhofer Institute for Solar Energy Systems ISE |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Helmers |first1=Henning |last2=Höhn |first2=Oliver |last3=Lackner |first3=David |last4=Schygulla |first4=Patrick |last5=Klitzke |first5=Malte |last6=Schön |first6=Jonas |last7=Pellegrino |first7=Carmine |last8=Oliva |first8=Eduard |last9=Schachtner |first9=Michael |last10=Beutel |first10=Paul |last11=Heckelmann |first11=Stefan |last12=Predan |first12=Felix |last13=Ohlmann |first13=Jens |last14=Siefer |first14=Gerald |last15=Dimroth |first15=Frank |chapter=Advancing solar energy conversion efficiency to 47.6% and exploring the spectral versatility of III-V photonic power converters |date=2024-03-08 |editor-last=Freundlich |editor-first=Alexandre |editor2-last=Hinzer |editor2-first=Karin |editor2-link=Karin Hinzer |editor3-last=Collin |editor3-first=Stéphane |editor4-last=Sellers |editor4-first=Ian R. |title=Physics, Simulation, and Photonic Engineering of Photovoltaic Devices XIII |chapter-url=https://www.spiedigitallibrary.org/conference-proceedings-of-spie/12881/3000352/Advancing-solar-energy-conversion-efficiency-to-476-and-exploring-the/10.1117/12.3000352.full |publisher=SPIE |page=36 |doi=10.1117/12.3000352 |isbn=978-1-5106-7022-8}}</ref>

==== GaInP/Si dual-junction solar cells ====
In 2016, a new approach was described for producing hybrid photovoltaic wafers combining the high efficiency of [[Multi-junction solar cell|III-V multi-junction solar cells]] with the economies and wealth of experience associated with silicon. The technical complications involved in growing the III-V material on silicon at the required high temperatures, a subject of study for some 30 years, are avoided by epitaxial growth of silicon on GaAs at low temperature by [[plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition]] (PECVD).<ref name="CariouChen2016">{{cite journal|last1=Cariou|first1=Romain|last2=Chen|first2=Wanghua|last3=Maurice|first3=Jean-Luc|last4=Yu|first4=Jingwen|last5=Patriarche|first5=Gilles|last6=Mauguin|first6=Olivia|last7=Largeau|first7=Ludovic|last8=Decobert|first8=Jean|last9=Roca i Cabarrocas|first9=Pere|title=Low temperature plasma enhanced CVD epitaxial growth of silicon on GaAs: a new paradigm for III-V/Si integration|journal=Scientific Reports|volume=6|year=2016|article-number=25674|issn=2045-2322|doi=10.1038/srep25674|doi-access=free|pmid=27166163|pmc=4863370|bibcode=2016NatSR...625674C}}</ref>

Si single-junction solar cells have been widely studied for decades and are reaching their practical efficiency of ~26% under 1-sun conditions.<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.1109/JPHOTOV.2014.2350695|title=Toward the Practical Limits of Silicon Solar Cells|journal=IEEE Journal of Photovoltaics|volume=4|issue=6|pages=1465–1469|year=2014|last1=Smith|first1=David D.|last2=Cousins|first2=Peter|last3=Westerberg|first3=Staffan|last4=Jesus-Tabajonda|first4=Russelle De|last5=Aniero|first5=Gerly|last6=Shen|first6=Yu-Chen|bibcode=2014IJPv....4.1465S |s2cid=33022605}}</ref> Increasing this efficiency may require adding more cells with bandgap energy larger than 1.1 eV to the Si cell, allowing to convert short-wavelength photons for generation of additional voltage. A dual-junction solar cell with a band gap of 1.6–1.8 eV as a top cell can reduce thermalization loss, produce a high external radiative efficiency and achieve theoretical efficiencies over 45%.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Almansouri|first1=Ibraheem|last2=Ho-Baillie|first2=Anita|author-link2=Anita Ho-Baillie|last3=Bremner|first3=Stephen P.|last4=Green|first4=Martin A.|year=2015|title=Supercharging Silicon Solar Cell Performance by Means of Multijunction Concept|journal=IEEE Journal of Photovoltaics|volume=5|issue=3|pages=968–976|doi=10.1109/JPHOTOV.2015.2395140|bibcode=2015IJPv....5..968A |s2cid=8477762}}</ref> A tandem cell can be fabricated by growing the GaInP and Si cells. Growing them separately can overcome the 4% lattice constant mismatch between Si and the most common III–V layers that prevent direct integration into one cell. The two cells therefore are separated by a transparent glass slide so the lattice mismatch does not cause strain to the system. This creates a cell with four electrical contacts and two junctions that demonstrated an efficiency of 18.1%. With a fill factor (FF) of 76.2%, the Si bottom cell reaches an efficiency of 11.7% (± 0.4) in the tandem device, resulting in a cumulative tandem cell efficiency of 29.8%.<ref>{{cite journal|doi= 10.1109/JPHOTOV.2016.2549746|title=Realization of GaInP/Si Dual-Junction Solar Cells with 29.8% 1-Sun Efficiency|journal=IEEE Journal of Photovoltaics|volume=6|issue=4|pages=1012–1019|year=2016|last1=Essig|first1=Stephanie|last2=Steiner|first2=Myles A.|last3=Allebe|first3=Christophe|last4=Geisz|first4=John F.|last5=Paviet-Salomon|first5=Bertrand|last6=Ward|first6=Scott|last7=Descoeudres|first7=Antoine|last8=Lasalvia|first8=Vincenzo|last9=Barraud|first9=Loris|last10=Badel|first10=Nicolas|last11=Faes|first11=Antonin|last12=Levrat|first12=Jacques|last13=Despeisse|first13=Matthieu|last14=Ballif|first14=Christophe|last15=Stradins|first15=Paul|last16=Young|first16=David L.|osti=1329999 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2016IJPv....6.1012E }}</ref> This efficiency exceeds the theoretical limit of 29.4%<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.1109/JPHOTOV.2013.2270351|title=Reassessment of the Limiting Efficiency for Crystalline Silicon Solar Cells|journal=IEEE Journal of Photovoltaics|volume=3|issue=4|pages=1184–1191|year=2013|last1=Richter|first1=Armin|last2=Hermle|first2=Martin|last3=Glunz|first3=Stefan W.|bibcode=2013IJPv....3.1184R |s2cid=6013813}}</ref> and the record experimental efficiency value of a Si 1-sun solar cell, and is also higher than the record-efficiency 1-sun GaAs device. However, using a GaAs substrate is expensive and not practical. Hence researchers try to make a cell with two electrical contact points and one junction, which does not need a GaAs substrate. This means there will be direct integration of GaInP and Si.

== Research in solar cells ==
{{See also|Solar cell research|Solar cell#History}}

=== Perovskite solar cells ===
{{Main|Perovskite solar cell}}
[[Perovskite solar cell]]s are solar cells that include a [[perovskite]]-structured material as the active layer. Most commonly, this is a solution-processed hybrid organic-inorganic tin or lead halide based material. Efficiencies have increased from below 5% at their first usage in 2009 to 25.5% in 2020, making them a very rapidly advancing technology and a hot topic in the solar cell field.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.nrel.gov/pv/assets/pdfs/best-research-cell-efficiencies.20200922.pdf |title=Best Research-Cell Efficiencies |url-status=live |access-date=28 September 2020 |archive-date=27 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201127183812/https://www.nrel.gov/pv/assets/pdfs/best-research-cell-efficiencies.20200922.pdf}}</ref> Researchers at [[University of Rochester]] reported in 2023 that significant further improvements in cell efficiency can be achieved by utilizing [[Purcell effect]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rochester.edu/newscenter/metal-perovskite-solar-cells-vs-silicon-efficiency-550212/ |title=Perovskites, a 'dirt cheap' alternative to silicon, just got a lot more efficient |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=February 16, 2023 |access-date=June 3, 2023 }}</ref>

Perovskite solar cells are also forecast to be extremely cheap to scale up, making them a very attractive option for commercialisation. So far most types of perovskite solar cells have not reached sufficient operational stability to be commercialised, although many research groups are investigating ways to solve this.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Kosasih|first1=Felix Utama|last2=Ducati|first2=Caterina|author-link2=Caterina Ducati|title=Characterising degradation of perovskite solar cells through in-situ and operando electron microscopy |journal=Nano Energy|date=May 2018|volume=47|pages=243–256 |doi=10.1016/j.nanoen.2018.02.055 |bibcode=2018NEne...47..243K |url=https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/handle/1810/275845|url-access=}}</ref> Energy and environmental sustainability of perovskite solar cells and tandem perovskite are shown to be dependent on the structures.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Tian|first1=Xueyu|last2=Stranks |first2=Samuel D.|last3=You|first3=Fengqi|date=July 2020|title=Life cycle energy use and environmental implications of high-performance perovskite tandem solar cells|journal=Science Advances|language=en|volume=6|issue=31 |article-number=eabb0055 |doi=10.1126/sciadv.abb0055|pmid=32789177|pmc=7399695 |bibcode=2020SciA....6...55T |issn=2375-2548|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Gong |first1=Jian |last2=Darling |first2=Seth B. |last3=You |first3=Fengqi |date=2015-07-03 |title=Perovskite photovoltaics: life-cycle assessment of energy and environmental impacts |journal=Energy & Environmental Science |language=en |volume=8 |issue=7 |pages=1953–1968 |doi=10.1039/C5EE00615E |bibcode=2015EnEnS...8.1953G |issn=1754-5706}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Tian |first1=Xueyu |last2=Stranks |first2=Samuel D. |last3=You |first3=Fengqi |date=2021-06-24 |title=Life cycle assessment of recycling strategies for perovskite photovoltaic modules |journal=Nature Sustainability |volume=4 |issue=9 |language=en |pages=821–829 |doi=10.1038/s41893-021-00737-z |bibcode=2021NatSu...4..821T |s2cid=235630649 |issn=2398-9629}}</ref> Photonic front contacts for light management can improve the perovskite cells' performance, via enhanced broadband absorption, while allowing better operational stability due to protection against the harmful high-energy (above Visible) radiation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Haque |first1=Sirazul |last2=Mendes |first2=Manuel J. |last3=Sanchez-Sobrado |first3=Olalla |last4=Águas |first4=Hugo |last5=Fortunato |first5=Elvira |last6=Martins |first6=Rodrigo |date=2019-05-01 |title=Photonic-structured TiO2 for high-efficiency, flexible and stable Perovskite solar cells |journal=Nano Energy |language=en |volume=59 |pages=91–101 |doi=10.1016/j.nanoen.2019.02.023 |s2cid=139461077 |issn=2211-2855|doi-access=free }}</ref> The inclusion of the toxic element [[lead]] in the most efficient perovskite solar cells is a potential problem for commercialisation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Li |first1=Junming |last2=Cao |first2=Hai-Lei |last3=Jiao |first3=Wen-Bin |last4=Wang |first4=Qiong |last5=Wei |first5=Mingdeng |last6=Cantone |first6=Irene |last7=Lü |first7=Jian |last8=Abate |first8=Antonio |date=2020-01-21 |title=Biological impact of lead from halide perovskites reveals the risk of introducing a safe threshold|journal=Nature Communications |language=en |volume=11 |issue=1 |page=310 |doi=10.1038/s41467-019-13910-y  |doi-access=free|pmid=31964862 |pmc=6974608 |bibcode=2020NatCo..11..310L |issn=2041-1723}}</ref>

=== Bifacial solar cells ===
{{Main|Bifacial solar cells}}
[[File:Noto Bifacial Plant.png|thumb|Bifacial solar cell plant in Noto (Senegal), 1988 − floor painted in white to enhance albedo]]

With a transparent rear side, bifacial solar cells can absorb light from both the front and rear sides. Hence, they can produce more electricity than conventional monofacial solar cells. The first patent of bifacial solar cells was filed by Japanese researcher Hiroshi Mori, in 1966.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 October 1961|author=Mori Hiroshi |title=Radiation energy transducing device|url=https://patents.google.com/patent/US3278811 |website=Google Patents}}</ref> Later, it is said that Russia was the first to deploy bifacial solar cells in their space program in the 1970s.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Koltun |first=M.M. |title=History of solar cell development in the Soviet space program and the terrestrial potential for this technology |journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells |volume=44 |issue=4 |pages=293–317 |year=1996 |doi=10.1016/S0927-0248(96)00046-3 |bibcode=1996SEMSC..44..293K }}</ref> In 1976, the [http://www.ies.upm.es/ Institute for Solar Energy] of the [[Technical University of Madrid]], began a research program for the development of bifacial solar cells led by [[Antonio Luque López|Prof. Antonio Luque]]. Based on 1977 US and Spanish patents by Luque, a practical bifacial cell was proposed with a front face as anode and a rear face as cathode; in previously reported proposals and attempts both faces were anodic and interconnection between cells was complicated and expensive.<ref>{{patent|ES|453575 (A1)|A. Luque: "Procedimiento para obtener células solares bifaciales" filing date 5 May 1977}}</ref><ref>{{patent|US|4169738 (A)|A. Luque: "Double-sided solar cell with self-refrigerating concentrator" filing date 21 November 1977}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Luque |first1=A. |last2=Cuevas |first2=A. |last3=Eguren |first3=J. |title=Solar-Cell Behavior under Variable Surface Recombination Velocity and Proposal of a Novel Structure |journal=Solid-State Electronics |date=1978 |volume=21 |issue=5 |pages=793–794 |doi=10.1016/0038-1101(78)90014-X |bibcode=1978SSEle..21..793L}}</ref> In 1980, Andrés Cuevas, a PhD student in Luque's team, demonstrated experimentally a 50% increase in output power of bifacial solar cells, relative to identically oriented and tilted monofacial ones, when a white background was provided.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Cuevas |first1=A. |last2=Luque |first2=A. |last3=Eguren |first3=J. |last4=Alamo |first4=J. del |title=50 Per cent more output power from an albedo-collecting flat panel using bifacial solar cells |journal=Solar Energy |date=1982 |volume=29 |issue=5 |pages=419–420 |doi=10.1016/0038-092x(82)90078-0 |bibcode=1982SoEn...29..419C}}</ref> In 1981 the company [[Isofoton]] was founded in [[Málaga]] to produce the developed bifacial cells, thus becoming the first industrialization of this PV cell technology. With an initial production capacity of 300&nbsp;kW/yr of bifacial solar cells, early landmarks of Isofoton's production were the 20kWp power plant in [[San Agustín de Guadalix]], built in 1986 for [[Iberdrola]], and an off grid installation by 1988 also of 20kWp in the village of Noto Gouye Diama ([[Senegal]]) funded by the [[Spanish Agency for International Development Cooperation|Spanish international aid and cooperation programs]].

Due to the reduced manufacturing cost, companies have again started to produce commercial bifacial modules since 2010. By 2017, there were at least eight certified PV manufacturers providing bifacial modules in North America. The International Technology Roadmap for Photovoltaics (ITRPV) predicted that the global market share of bifacial technology will expand from less than 5% in 2016 to 30% in 2027.<ref>{{Cite web|title=International Technology Roadmap for Photovoltaic (ITRPV) – Home |website=www.itrpv.net|url=http://www.itrpv.net/|access-date=20 February 2018}}</ref>

Due to the significant interest in the bifacial technology, a recent study has investigated the performance and optimization of bifacial solar modules worldwide.<ref name="Sun-2018">{{Cite journal |last1=Sun |first1=Xingshu |last2=Khan |first2=Mohammad Ryyan |last3=Deline |first3=Chris |last4=Alam |first4=Muhammad Ashraful |title=Optimization and performance of bifacial solar modules: A global perspective |journal=Applied Energy |year=2018 |volume=212 |pages=1601–1610 |arxiv=1709.10026 |s2cid=117375370 |doi=10.1016/j.apenergy.2017.12.041|bibcode=2018ApEn..212.1601S }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Khan|first1=M. Ryyan |last2=Hanna |first2=Amir |last3=Sun |first3=Xingshu |last4=Alam |first4=Muhammad A. |title=Vertical bifacial solar farms: Physics, design, and global optimization |journal=Applied Energy |volume=206 |pages=240–248 |year=2017 |arxiv=1704.08630 |bibcode=2017ApEn..206..240K |s2cid=115039440 |doi=10.1016/j.apenergy.2017.08.042}}</ref> The results indicate that, across the globe, ground-mounted bifacial modules can only offer ~10% gain in annual electricity yields compared to the monofacial counterparts for [[Albedo|a ground albedo coefficient]] of 25% (typical for concrete and vegetation groundcovers). However, the gain can be increased to ~30% by elevating the module 1&nbsp;m above the ground and enhancing the ground albedo coefficient to 50%. Sun ''et al.'' also derived a set of empirical equations that can optimize bifacial solar modules analytically.<ref name="Sun-2018" /> In addition, there is evidence that bifacial panels work better than traditional panels in snowy environments as bifacial cells on dual-axis trackers made 14% more electricity in a year than their monofacial counterparts and 40% during the peak winter months.<ref>Burnham, Performance of Bifacial Photovoltaic Modules on a Dual-Axis Tracker in a High-Latitude, High-Albedo Environment, 2019 IEEE 46th Photovoltaic Specialists Conference (PVSC), Chicago, IL, USA, 2019, pp. 1320-1327.</ref>

[https://nanohub.org/tools/pub An online simulation tool] is available to model the performance of bifacial modules in any arbitrary location across the entire world. It can also optimize bifacial modules as a function of tilt angle, azimuth angle, and elevation above the ground.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Zhao |first1=Binglin |last2=Sun |first2=Xingshu |last3=Khan |first3=Mohammad Ryyan |last4=Alam |first4=Muhammad Ashraful |date=19 February 2018 |title=Purdue Bifacial Module Calculator |website=nanoHUB |doi=10.4231/d3542jb3c}}</ref>

=== Intermediate band ===
{{Main|Intermediate band photovoltaics}}

'''Intermediate band photovoltaics''' in solar cell research provides methods for exceeding the [[Shockley–Queisser limit]] on the efficiency of a cell. It introduces an intermediate band (IB) energy level in between the valence and conduction bands. Theoretically, introducing an IB allows two [[photons]] with energy less than the [[bandgap]] to excite an electron from the [[valence band]] to the [[conduction band]]. This increases the induced photocurrent and thereby efficiency.<ref>{{cite journal |doi=10.1103/PhysRevLett.78.5014|title=Increasing the Efficiency of Ideal Solar Cells by Photon Induced Transitions at Intermediate Levels |journal=Physical Review Letters |volume=78 |issue=26 |pages=5014–5017 |year=1997 |last1=Luque |first1=Antonio |last2=Martí |first2=Antonio |bibcode=1997PhRvL..78.5014L}}</ref>

[[Antonio Luque López|Luque]] and Marti first derived a theoretical limit for an IB device with one midgap energy level using [[detailed balance]]. They assumed no carriers were collected at the IB and that the device was under full concentration. They found the IB maximum efficiency to be 63.2%, for a bandgap of 1.95eV with the IB 0.71eV from either the valence or conduction band ans compared to the under one sun illumination limiting efficiency of 47%.<ref>{{cite book |author=Okada, Yoshitaka |author2=Tomah Sogabe |author3=Yasushi Shoji|doi=10.1039/9781849739955-00425|isbn= 978-1-84973-995-5|chapter=Ch. 13: Intermediate Band Solar Cells|title= Advanced Concepts in Photovoltaics |editor=Arthur J. Nozik |editor2=Gavin Conibeer |editor3=Matthew C. Beard|volume=11|place= Cambridge, UK|publisher= Royal Society of Chemistry|year= 2014|pages= 425–54|series=Energy and Environment Series}}</ref> Several means are under study to realize IB semiconductors with such optimum 3-bandgap configuration, namely via materials engineering (controlled inclusion of deep level impurities or highly mismatched alloys) and nano-structuring (quantum-dots in host hetero-crystals).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ramiro |first1=Iñigo |last2=Martí |first2=Antonio |date=July 2021 |title=Intermediate band solar cells: Present and future |journal=Progress in Photovoltaics: Research and Applications |language=en |volume=29 |issue=7 |pages=705–713 |doi=10.1002/pip.3351 |s2cid=226335202 |issn=1062-7995|url=https://oa.upm.es/64919/2/IBSC_present_and_future_PIP3351.pdf |url-access= }}</ref>

=== Liquid inks ===
In 2014, researchers at [[California NanoSystems Institute]] discovered using [[kesterite]] and [[perovskite]] improved [[electric power conversion]] efficiency for solar cells.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://phys.org/news/2014-09-liquid-inks-solar-cells.html |title=Researchers use liquid inks to create better solar cells |website=Phys.org |date=17 September 2014 |first=Shaun |last=Mason}}</ref>

In December 2022, it was reported that [[Massachusetts Institute of Technology|MIT]] researchers had developed ultralight fabric solar cells. These cells offer a weight one-hundredth that of traditional panels while generating 18 times more power per kilogram. Thinner than a human hair, these cells can be laminated onto various surfaces, such as boat sails, tents, tarps, or drone wings, to extend their functionality. Using ink-based materials and scalable techniques, researchers coat the solar cell structure with printable electronic inks, completing the module with [[screen-printed electrodes]]. Tested on high-strength fabric, the cells produce 370 watts-per-kilogram, representing an improvement over conventional solar cells.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-12-16 |title=This thin solar cell can turn any surface into an energy source |url=https://www.weforum.org/agenda/2022/12/thin-solar-cell-surface-energy-source-technology-science/ |website=World Economic Forum}}</ref>

===Upconversion and downconversion===
[[Photon upconversion]] is the process of using two low-energy (''e.g.'', infrared) photons to produce one higher energy photon; '''downconversion''' is the process of using one high energy photon (''e.g.'', ultraviolet) to produce two lower energy photons. Either of these techniques could be used to produce higher efficiency solar cells by allowing solar photons to be more efficiently used. The difficulty, however, is that the conversion efficiency of existing [[phosphor]]s exhibiting up- or down-conversion is low, and is typically narrow band.

One upconversion technique is to incorporate [[lanthanide]]-doped materials ([[Erbium|{{chem|Er|3+}}]], [[Ytterbium|{{chem|Yb|3+}}]], [[Holmium|{{chem|Ho|3+}}]] or a combination), taking advantage of their [[luminescence]] to convert [[infrared radiation]] to visible light. Upconversion process occurs when two [[infrared]] photons are absorbed by [[rare-earth]] [[ion]]s to generate a (high-energy) absorbable photon. As example, the energy transfer upconversion process (ETU), consists in successive transfer processes between excited ions in the near infrared. The upconverter material could be placed below the solar cell to absorb the infrared light that passes through the silicon. Useful ions are most commonly found in the trivalent state. {{chem|Er|+}} ions have been the most used. {{chem|Er|3+}} ions absorb solar radiation around 1.54&nbsp;μm. Two {{chem|Er|3+}} ions that have absorbed this radiation can interact with each other through an upconversion process. The excited ion emits light above the Si bandgap that is absorbed by the solar cell and creates an additional electron–hole pair that can generate current. However, the increased efficiency was small. In addition, [[fluoroindate]] glasses have low [[phonon]] energy and have been proposed as suitable matrix doped with {{chem|Ho|3+}} ions.<ref>{{cite journal|author1=Hernández-Rodríguez, M.A. |author2=Imanieh, M.H. |author3=Martín, L.L. |author4= Martín, I.R. |title=Experimental enhancement of the photocurrent in a solar cell using upconversion process in fluoroindate glasses exciting at 1480nm|journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells|date=September 2013 |volume=116 |pages=171–175 |doi=10.1016/j.solmat.2013.04.023|bibcode=2013SEMSC.116..171H }}</ref>

=== Light-absorbing dyes ===
{{Main|Dye-sensitized solar cell|l1=Dye-sensitized solar cells}}
[[Dye-sensitized solar cell]]s (DSSCs) are made of low-cost materials and do not need elaborate manufacturing equipment, so they can be made in a [[Do it yourself|DIY]] fashion. In bulk it should be significantly less expensive than older [[Solid state (electronics)|solid-state]] cell designs. DSSC's can be engineered into flexible sheets and although its [[conversion efficiency]] is less than the best [[thin film cell]]s, its [[price/performance ratio]] may be high enough to allow them to compete with [[fossil fuel electrical generation]].

Typically a [[ruthenium]] [[Metalorganics|metalorganic]] [[dye]] (Ru-centered) is used as a [[monolayer]] of light-absorbing material, which is adsorbed onto a thin film of [[Titanium dioxide nanoparticle|titanium dioxide]]. The dye-sensitized solar cell depends on this [[mesoporous]] layer of [[nanoparticulate]] [[titanium dioxide]] (TiO<sub>2</sub>) to greatly amplify the surface area (200–300 m<sup>2</sup>/g {{chem|TiO|2}}, as compared to approximately 10 m<sup>2</sup>/g of flat single crystal) which allows for a greater number of dyes per solar cell area (which in term in increases the current). The photogenerated electrons from the light absorbing dye are passed on to the n-type {{chem|TiO|2}} and the holes are absorbed by an [[electrolyte]] on the other side of the dye. The circuit is completed by a [[redox]] couple in the electrolyte, which can be liquid or solid. This type of cell allows more flexible use of materials and is typically manufactured by [[screen printing]] or [[ultrasonic nozzle]]s, with the potential for lower processing costs than those used for bulk solar cells. However, the dyes in these cells also suffer from [[chemical decomposition|degradation]] under heat and [[UV]] light and the cell casing is difficult to [[Seal (mechanical)|seal]] due to the solvents used in assembly. Due to this reason, researchers have developed solid-state dye-sensitized solar cells that use a solid electrolyte to avoid leakage.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wang |first1=Peng |last2=Zakeeruddin |first2=Shaik M. |last3=Moser |first3=Jacques E. |last4=Nazeeruddin |first4=Mohammad K. |last5=Sekiguchi |first5=Takashi |last6=Grätzel |first6=Michael |date=June 2003 |title=A stable quasi-solid-state dye-sensitized solar cell with an amphiphilic ruthenium sensitizer and polymer gel electrolyte |journal=Nature Materials |language=en |volume=2 |issue=6 |pages=402–407 |doi=10.1038/nmat904 |pmid=12754500 |bibcode=2003NatMa...2..402W |s2cid=27383758 |issn=1476-4660|doi-access=free }}</ref> The first commercial shipment of DSSC solar modules occurred in July 2009 from G24i Innovations.<ref>{{usurped|1=[https://web.archive.org/web/20061029004539/http://www.g24i.com/ Dye Sensitized Solar Cells]}}. G24i.com (2 April 2014). Retrieved 20 April 2014.</ref>

=== Quantum dots ===
{{Main|Quantum dot solar cell}}
[[Quantum dot solar cell]]s (QDSCs) are based on the Gratzel cell, or [[dye-sensitized solar cell]] architecture, but employ low [[band gap]] [[semiconductor]] [[nanoparticle]]s, fabricated with crystallite sizes small enough to form [[quantum dots]] (such as [[cadmium sulfide|CdS]], [[cadmium selenide|CdSe]], [[Stibnite|{{chem|Sb|2|S|3}}]], [[Lead(II) sulfide|PbS]], etc.), instead of organic or organometallic dyes as light absorbers. Due to the toxicity associated with Cd and Pb based compounds there are also a series of "green" QD sensitizing materials in development (such as {{chem2|CuInS2}}, {{chem2|CuInSe2}}, and {{chem2|CuInSeS}}).<ref>{{Cite journal|date=1 October 2016|title=Quantum dot sensitized solar cell: Recent advances and future perspectives in photoanode|journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells|volume=155|pages=294–322|doi=10.1016/j.solmat.2016.05.062|issn=0927-0248|last1=Sharma|first1=Darshan|last2=Jha|first2=Ranjana|last3=Kumar|first3=Shiv|bibcode=2016SEMSC.155..294S }}</ref> QD's size quantization allows for the band gap to be tuned by simply changing particle size. They also have high [[Refractive index|extinction coefficients]] and have shown the possibility of [[multiple exciton generation]].<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.1126/science.1209845|title=Peak External Photocurrent Quantum Efficiency Exceeding 100% via MEG in a Quantum Dot Solar Cell|year=2011|last1=Semonin|first1=O. E.|last2=Luther|first2=J. M.|last3=Choi|first3=S.|last4=Chen|first4=H.-Y.|last5=Gao|first5=J.|last6=Nozik|first6=A. J.|last7=Beard|first7=M. C.|journal=Science|volume=334|issue=6062|pages=1530–3|pmid=22174246 |bibcode=2011Sci...334.1530S|s2cid=36022754}}</ref>

In a QDSC, a [[mesoporous]] layer of [[titanium dioxide]] nanoparticles forms the backbone of the cell, much like in a DSSC. This {{chem|TiO|2}} layer can then be made photoactive by coating with semiconductor quantum dots using [[chemical bath deposition]], [[electrophoretic deposition]] or successive ionic layer adsorption and reaction. The electrical circuit is then completed through the use of a liquid or solid [[redox couple]]. The efficiency of QDSCs has increased<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.1021/ar200315d|pmid=22493938|title=Boosting the Efficiency of Quantum Dot Sensitized Solar Cells through Modulation of Interfacial Charge Transfer|year=2012|last1=Kamat|first1=Prashant V.|journal=Accounts of Chemical Research|volume=45|issue=11|pages=1906–15 |bibcode=2012AcChR..45.1906K }}</ref> to over 5% shown for both liquid-junction<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.1021/ja211224s|title=Mn-Doped Quantum Dot Sensitized Solar Cells: A Strategy to Boost Efficiency over 5%|year=2012|last1=Santra|first1=Pralay K.|last2=Kamat|first2=Prashant V.|journal=Journal of the American Chemical Society|volume=134|issue=5|pages=2508–11|pmid=22280479|bibcode=2012JAChS.134.2508S }}</ref> and solid state cells,<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.1021/jz100308q|title=Sb2S3-Based Mesoscopic Solar Cell using an Organic Hole Conductor|year=2010|last1=Moon|first1=Soo-Jin|last2=Itzhaik|first2=Yafit|last3=Yum|first3=Jun-Ho|last4=Zakeeruddin|first4=Shaik M.|last5=Hodes|first5=Gary|last6=GräTzel|first6=Michael|journal=The Journal of Physical Chemistry Letters|volume=1|issue=10|page=1524 |bibcode=2010JPCL....1.1524M }}</ref> with a reported peak efficiency of 11.91%.<ref>{{cite journal|pmid=26962680|doi=10.1021/jacs.6b00615|title=Zn–Cu–In–Se Quantum Dot Solar Cells with a Certified Power Conversion Efficiency of 11.6%|journal=Journal of the American Chemical Society|volume=138|issue=12|pages=4201–4209|year=2016|last1=Du|first1=Jun|last2=Du|first2=Zhonglin|last3=Hu|first3=Jin-Song|last4=Pan|first4=Zhenxiao|last5=Shen|first5=Qing|last6=Sun|first6=Jiankun|last7=Long|first7=Donghui|last8=Dong|first8=Hui|last9=Sun|first9=Litao|last10=Zhong|first10=Xinhua|last11=Wan|first11=Li-Jun|bibcode=2016JAChS.138.4201D }}</ref> In an effort to decrease production costs, the [[Prashant V. Kamat|Prashant Kamat]] research group<ref>[https://nd.edu/~kamatlab/research_solarCells.html Solar Cell Research {{!}}{{!}} The Prashant Kamat lab at the University of Notre Dame]. Nd.edu (22 February 2007). Retrieved 17 May 2012.</ref> demonstrated a solar paint made with {{chem|TiO|2}} and CdSe that can be applied using a one-step method to any conductive surface with efficiencies over 1%.<ref>{{cite journal|doi=10.1021/nn204381g|title=Sun-BelievableSolar Paint. A Transformative One-Step Approach for Designing Nanocrystalline Solar Cells|year=2012|last1=Genovese|first1=Matthew P.|last2=Lightcap|first2=Ian V.|last3=Kamat|first3=Prashant V.|journal=ACS Nano|volume=6|pages=865–72|pmid=22147684|issue=1 |bibcode=2012ACSNa...6..865G }}</ref> However, the absorption of quantum dots (QDs) in QDSCs is weak at room temperature.<ref name="Yu-2017">{{Cite journal|last1=Yu|first1=Peng|last2=Wu|first2=Jiang|last3=Gao|first3=Lei|last4=Liu|first4=Huiyun|last5=Wang|first5=Zhiming|date=1 March 2017|title=InGaAs and GaAs quantum dot solar cells grown by droplet epitaxy|journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells|volume=161|pages=377–381|doi=10.1016/j.solmat.2016.12.024|bibcode=2017SEMSC.161..377Y |url=https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/1539114/1/Accepted%20QDSC%20droplet%20epitaxy.pdf|url-access=}}</ref> The [[Plasmonic solar cell|plasmonic nanoparticles]] can be utilized to address the weak absorption of QDs (e.g., nanostars).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wu|first1=Jiang|last2=Yu|first2=Peng|last3=Susha|first3=Andrei S.|last4=Sablon|first4=Kimberly A.|last5=Chen|first5=Haiyuan|last6=Zhou|first6=Zhihua|last7=Li|first7=Handong|last8=Ji|first8=Haining|last9=Niu|first9=Xiaobin|date=1 April 2015|title=Broadband efficiency enhancement in quantum dot solar cells coupled with multispiked plasmonic nanostars|journal=Nano Energy|volume=13|pages=827–835|doi=10.1016/j.nanoen.2015.02.012|bibcode=2015NEne...13..827W |s2cid=98282021 }}</ref> Adding an external infrared pumping source to excite intraband and interband transition of QDs is another solution.<ref name="Yu-2017" />

=== Organic/polymer solar cells ===
{{Main|Organic solar cell|Polymer solar cell}}
[[Organic solar cell]]s and [[polymer solar cell]]s are built from thin films (typically 100&nbsp;nm) of [[organic semiconductor]]s including polymers, such as [[polyphenylene vinylene]] and small-molecule compounds like copper phthalocyanine (a blue or green organic pigment) and [[fullerene|carbon fullerenes]] and fullerene derivatives such as [[PCBM]].

They can be processed from liquid solution, offering the possibility of a simple roll-to-roll printing process, potentially leading to inexpensive, large-scale production. In addition, these cells could be beneficial for some applications where mechanical flexibility and disposability are important. Current cell efficiencies are, however, very low, and practical devices are essentially non-existent.

Energy conversion efficiencies achieved to date using conductive polymers are very low compared to inorganic materials. However, [[Konarka]] Power Plastic reached efficiency of 8.3%<ref>[http://www.pv-tech.org/news/nrel_validates_konarkas_8.3_power_plastic_efficiency_record Konarka Power Plastic reaches 8.3% efficiency]. pv-tech.org. Retrieved 7 May 2011.</ref> and organic tandem cells in 2012 reached 11.1%.{{citation needed|date=June 2014}}

The active region of an organic device consists of two materials, one electron donor and one electron acceptor. When a photon is converted into an electron hole pair, typically in the donor material, the charges tend to remain bound in the form of an [[exciton]], separating when the exciton diffuses to the donor-acceptor interface, unlike most other solar cell types. The short exciton diffusion lengths of most polymer systems tend to limit the efficiency of such devices. Nanostructured interfaces, sometimes in the form of bulk heterojunctions, can improve performance.<ref>{{Cite journal| doi=10.1016/S1369-7021(07)70276-6| title=Polymer-based solar cells| journal=Materials Today| volume=10| issue=11| page=28| year=2007| last1=Mayer| first1=A.| last2=Scully| first2=S.| last3=Hardin| first3=B.| last4=Rowell| first4=M.| last5=McGehee| first5=M.| doi-access=free| bibcode=2007MaTod..10...28M}}</ref>

In 2011, MIT and Michigan State researchers developed solar cells with a power efficiency close to 2% with a transparency to the human eye greater than 65%, achieved by selectively absorbing the ultraviolet and near-infrared parts of the spectrum with small-molecule compounds.<ref>{{Cite journal| doi=10.1063/1.3567516| title = Transparent, near-infrared organic photovoltaic solar cells for window and energy-scavenging applications| journal=Applied Physics Letters| volume=98| issue=11| page=113305| year=2011| last1=Lunt| first1=R. R.| last2=Bulovic| first2=V.| bibcode=2011ApPhL..98k3305L| doi-access=free| hdl=1721.1/71948| hdl-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Rudolf|first=John Collins|date=20 April 2011 |title=Transparent Photovoltaic Cells Turn Windows into Solar Panels |website=green.blogs.nytimes.com |url=http://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/04/20/transparent-photovoltaic-cells-turn-windows-into-solar-panels/}}</ref> Researchers at UCLA more recently developed an analogous polymer solar cell, following the same approach, that is 70% transparent and has a 4% power conversion efficiency.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.enviro-news.com/news/ucla-scientists-develop-transparent-solar-cell.html|title=UCLA Scientists Develop Transparent Solar Cell|publisher=Enviro-News.com |date=24 July 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120727005130/http://www.enviro-news.com/news/ucla-scientists-develop-transparent-solar-cell.html|archive-date=27 July 2012}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal| doi=10.1002/adma.201103404| pmid=22057647| title=Practical Roadmap and Limits to Nanostructured Photovoltaics| journal=Advanced Materials| volume=23| issue=48| pages=5712–27| year=2011| last1=Lunt| first1=R. R.| last2=Osedach| first2=T. P.| last3=Brown| first3=P. R.| last4=Rowehl| first4=J. A.| last5=Bulović| first5=V.| bibcode=2011AdM....23.5712L| hdl=1721.1/80286| s2cid=13511794| hdl-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal| doi=10.1063/1.4738896| title=Theoretical limits for visibly transparent photovoltaics| journal=Applied Physics Letters| volume=101| issue=4| page=043902| year=2012| last1=Lunt| first1=R. R.| bibcode=2012ApPhL.101d3902L}}</ref> These lightweight, flexible cells can be produced in bulk at a low cost and could be used to create power generating windows.

In 2013, researchers announced [[polymer solar cell|polymer cells]] with some 3% efficiency. They used [[block copolymers]], self-assembling organic materials that arrange themselves into distinct layers. The research focused on P3HT-b-PFTBT that separates into bands some 16 nanometers wide.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Guo |first1=C. |last2=Lin |first2=Y. H. |last3=Witman |first3=M. D. |last4=Smith |first4=K. A. |last5=Wang |first5=C. |last6=Hexemer |first6=A. |last7=Strzalka |first7=J. |last8=Gomez |first8=E. D. |last9=Verduzco |first9=R. |title=Conjugated Block Copolymer Photovoltaics with near 3% Efficiency through Microphase Separation |journal=Nano Letters |volume=13 |issue=6 |pages=2957–63 |year=2013 |pmid=23687903 |bibcode=2013NanoL..13.2957G |url= https://figshare.com/articles/Conjugated_Block_Copolymer_Photovoltaics_with_near_3_Efficiency_through_Microphase_Separation/2406439 |doi=10.1021/nl401420s}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Organic polymers create new class of solar energy devices |date=31 May 2013 |publisher=Kurzweil Accelerating Institute |url=http://www.kurzweilai.net/organic-polymers-create-new-class-of-solar-energy-devices |access-date=1 June 2013}}</ref>

=== Adaptive cells ===
Adaptive cells change their absorption/reflection characteristics depending on environmental conditions. An adaptive material responds to the intensity and angle of incident light. At the part of the cell where the light is most intense, the cell surface changes from reflective to adaptive, allowing the light to penetrate the cell. The other parts of the cell remain reflective increasing the retention of the absorbed light within the cell.<ref name=tr1407>Bullis, Kevin (30 July 2014) [http://www.technologyreview.com/news/529476/adaptive-material-could-cut-the-cost-of-solar-in-half Adaptive Material Could Cut the Cost of Solar in Half]. ''MIT Technology Review''</ref>

In 2014, a system was developed that combined an adaptive surface with a glass substrate that redirect the absorbed to a light absorber on the edges of the sheet. The system also includes an array of fixed lenses/mirrors to concentrate light onto the adaptive surface. As the day continues, the concentrated light moves along the surface of the cell. That surface switches from reflective to adaptive when the light is most concentrated and back to reflective after the light moves along.<ref name=tr1407/>

=== Surface texturing ===
[[File:SolarImpulse HB-SIA landing Brussels Airport 3-crop.jpg|thumb|upright=1.1|[[Solar Impulse]] aircraft are Swiss-designed single-seat monoplanes powered entirely from photovoltaic cells.]]

Incident light rays onto a textured surface do not reflect out to the air as opposed to rays onto a flat surface, but rather some light rays are bounced back onto the other surface again due to the geometry of the surface; increasing light absorption and light to electricity conversion efficiency. Surface texturing is one technique used to reduce optical losses, primarily in cost-effective, low light absorption [[thin-film solar cells]]. In combination with [[anti-reflective coating]], surface texturing technique can effectively trap light rays within a thin film silicon solar cell. Consequently, at the same power output, thickness for solar cells can decrease with the increased absorption of light rays.

Surface texture geometry and texturing techniques can be done in multiple ways. Etching [[Crystalline silicon|c-Si]] substrates can produce randomly distributed square based pyramids on the surface using anisotropic etchants.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Campbell|first=Patrick|author2=Green, Martin A. |title=Light Trapping Properties of Pyramidally textured surfaces|journal=Journal of Applied Physics|date=Feb 1987|volume=62|issue=1| pages=243–249|doi=10.1063/1.339189|bibcode=1987JAP....62..243C}}</ref> Studies show that [[Crystalline silicon|c-Si]] wafers could be etched down to form nano-scale inverted pyramids. In 2012, researchers at MIT reported that c-Si films textured with nanoscale inverted pyramids could achieve light absorption comparable to 30 times thicker planar c-Si.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Mavrokefalos |first=Anastassios |author2=Han, Sang Eon. |author3=Yerci, Selcuk |author4=Branham, M.S. |author5=Chen, Gang. |date=June 2012 |title=Efficient Light Trapping in Inverted Nanopyramid Thin Crystalline Silicon Membranes for Solar Cell Applications |journal=Nano Letters |volume=12 |issue=6 |pages=2792–2796 |bibcode=2012NanoL..12.2792M |doi=10.1021/nl2045777 |pmid=22612694 |s2cid=18134294 |hdl-access=free |hdl=1721.1/86899}}</ref> While easier to manufacture, but with less efficiency, multicrystalline solar cells can be surface-textured through isotopic etching or photolithography methods to yield solar energy conversion efficiency comparable to that of monocrystalline silicon cells.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Zhao|first=Jianhua|author2=Wang, Aihua |author3=Green, Martin A.|title=19.8% efficient "honeycomb" textured multicrystalline and 24.4% monocrystalline silicon solar cells|journal=Applied Physics Letters|date=May 1998|volume=73|issue=14| pages=1991–1993|doi=10.1063/1.122345|bibcode=1998ApPhL..73.1991Z}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last=Hauser|first=H.|author2=Michl, B.|author3=Kubler, V.|author4= Schwarzkopf, S.|author5= Muller, C.|author6= Hermle, M.|author7= Blasi, B.|title=Nanoimprint Lithography for Honeycomb Texturing of Multicrystalline Silicon|journal=Energy Procedia|date=2011|volume=8|pages=648–653|doi=10.1016/j.egypro.2011.06.196|doi-access=free|bibcode=2011EnPro...8..648H }}</ref>

This texture effect as well as the interaction with other interfaces in the PV module is a challenging optical simulation task, but at least one efficient method for modeling and optimization that exists is the [[OPTOS formalism]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Tucher|first1=Nico|last2=Eisenlohr|first2=Johannes|last3=Gebrewold|first3=Habtamu|last4=Kiefel|first4=Peter|last5=Höhn|first5=Oliver|last6=Hauser|first6=Hubert|last7=Goldschmidt|first7=Jan Christoph|last8=Bläsi|first8=Benedikt|date=11 July 2016|title=Optical simulation of photovoltaic modules with multiple textured interfaces using the matrix-based formalism OPTOS|journal=Optics Express|volume=24|issue=14|pages=A1083–A1093|doi=10.1364/OE.24.0A1083|pmid=27410896|bibcode=2016OExpr..24A1083T|doi-access=free}}</ref>

=== Encapsulation ===
Solar cells are commonly encapsulated in a transparent polymeric resin to protect the delicate solar cell regions for coming into contact with moisture, dirt, ice, and other environmental conditions expected during operation. Encapsulants are commonly made from [[polyvinyl acetate]] or glass. Most encapsulants are uniform in structure and composition, which increases light collection owing to light trapping from total internal reflection of light within the resin. Research has been conducted into structuring the encapsulant to provide further collection of light. Such encapsulants have included roughened glass surfaces,<ref>{{cite book|doi=10.1109/PVSC.2010.5614568|chapter=Light management for reduction of bus bar and gridline shadowing in photovoltaic modules|title=2010 35th IEEE Photovoltaic Specialists Conference|page=000979|year=2010|last1=Jaus|first1=J.|last2=Pantsar|first2=H.|last3=Eckert|first3=J.|last4=Duell|first4=M.|last5=Herfurth|first5=H.|last6=Doble|first6=D.|isbn=978-1-4244-5890-5|s2cid=30512545}}</ref> diffractive elements,<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Mingareev|first1=I.|last2=Berlich|first2=R.|last3=Eichelkraut|first3=T. J.|last4=Herfurth|first4=H.|last5=Heinemann|first5=S.|last6=Richardson|first6=M. C.|date=6 June 2011|title=Diffractive optical elements utilized for efficiency enhancement of photovoltaic modules|journal=Optics Express|volume=19|issue=12|pages=11397–404|doi=10.1364/OE.19.011397|pmid=21716370|bibcode=2011OExpr..1911397M|url=https://stars.library.ucf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2671&context=facultybib2010|doi-access=free}}</ref> prism arrays,<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Uematsu|first1=T|last2=Yazawa|first2=Y|last3=Miyamura|first3=Y|last4=Muramatsu|first4=S|last5=Ohtsuka|first5=H|last6=Tsutsui|first6=K|last7=Warabisako|first7=T|date=1 March 2001|title=Static concentrator photovoltaic module with prism array|journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells|series=PVSEC 11 – PART III|volume=67|issue=1–4|pages=415–423|doi=10.1016/S0927-0248(00)00310-X|bibcode=2001SEMSC..67..415U}}</ref> air prisms,<ref name="Chen-2016">{{Cite journal|last1=Chen|first1=Fu-hao|last2=Pathreeker|first2=Shreyas|last3=Kaur|first3=Jaspreet|last4=Hosein|first4=Ian D.|date=31 October 2016|title=Increasing light capture in silicon solar cells with encapsulants incorporating air prisms to reduce metallic contact losses|journal=Optics Express|volume=24|issue=22|pages=A1419–A1430|doi=10.1364/oe.24.0a1419|pmid=27828526|bibcode=2016OExpr..24A1419C|doi-access=free}}</ref> v-grooves,<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Korech|first1=Omer|last2=Gordon|first2=Jeffrey M.|last3=Katz|first3=Eugene A.|last4=Feuermann|first4=Daniel|last5=Eisenberg|first5=Naftali|date=1 October 2007|title=Dielectric microconcentrators for efficiency enhancement in concentrator solar cells|journal=Optics Letters|volume=32|issue=19|pages=2789–91|doi=10.1364/OL.32.002789|pmid=17909574|bibcode=2007OptL...32.2789K}}</ref> diffuse elements, as well as multi-directional waveguide arrays.<ref>{{Cite book|last1=Hosein|first1=Ian D.|last2=Lin|first2=Hao|last3=Ponte|first3=Matthew R.|last4=Basker|first4=Dinesh K.|last5=Saravanamuttu|first5=Kalaichelvi|title=Renewable Energy and the Environment |chapter=Enhancing Solar Energy Light Capture with Multi-Directional Waveguide Lattices |date=3 November 2013 |doi=10.1364/OSE.2013.RM2D.2 |article-number=RM2D.2|isbn=978-1-55752-986-2}}</ref> Prism arrays show an overall 5% increase in the total solar energy conversion.<ref name="Chen-2016" /> Arrays of vertically aligned broadband waveguides provide a 10% increase at normal incidence, as well as wide-angle collection enhancement of up to 4%,<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Biria|first1=Saeid|last2=Chen|first2=Fu Hao|last3=Pathreeker|first3=Shreyas|last4=Hosein|first4=Ian D.|date=22 December 2017|title=Polymer Encapsulants Incorporating Light-Guiding Architectures to Increase Optical Energy Conversion in Solar Cells|journal=Advanced Materials|volume=30|issue=8|article-number=1705382|doi=10.1002/adma.201705382|pmid=29271510|s2cid=3368811 }}</ref> with optimized structures yielding up to a 20% increase in short circuit current.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Biria|first1=Saeid|last2=Chen|first2=Fu-Hao|last3=Hosein|first3=Ian D.|title=Enhanced Wide-Angle Energy Conversion Using Structure-Tunable Waveguide Arrays as Encapsulation Materials for Silicon Solar Cells|journal=Physica Status Solidi A|issue=2|article-number=1800716|doi=10.1002/pssa.201800716|year=2019|volume=216|bibcode=2019PSSAR.21600716B|s2cid=125253775}}</ref> Active coatings that convert infrared light into visible light have shown a 30% increase.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Huang|first1=Zhiyuan|last2=Li|first2=Xin|last3=Mahboub|first3=Melika|last4=Hanson|first4=Kerry M.|last5=Nichols|first5=Valerie M.|last6=Le|first6=Hoang|last7=Tang|first7=Ming L.|last8=Bardeen|first8=Christopher J.|date=12 August 2015|title=Hybrid Molecule–Nanocrystal Photon Upconversion Across the Visible and Near-Infrared|journal=Nano Letters|volume=15|issue=8|pages=5552–5557|doi=10.1021/acs.nanolett.5b02130|pmid=26161875|bibcode=2015NanoL..15.5552H|doi-access=free|url=https://escholarship.org/content/qt1bb3z0sc/qt1bb3z0sc.pdf?t=ozic6b}}</ref> Nanoparticle coatings inducing plasmonic light scattering increase wide-angle conversion efficiency up to 3%. Optical structures have also been created in encapsulation materials to effectively "cloak" the metallic front contacts.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Schumann|first1=Martin F.|last2=Langenhorst|first2=Malte|last3=Smeets|first3=Michael|last4=Ding|first4=Kaining|last5=Paetzold|first5=Ulrich W.|last6=Wegener|first6=Martin|date=4 July 2017|title=All-Angle Invisibility Cloaking of Contact Fingers on Solar Cells by Refractive Free-Form Surfaces|journal=Advanced Optical Materials|volume=5|issue=17|article-number=1700164|doi=10.1002/adom.201700164|s2cid=102931532 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|date=1 August 2018|title=Freeform surface invisibility cloaking of interconnection lines in thin-film photovoltaic modules|journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells|volume=182|pages=294–301|doi=10.1016/j.solmat.2018.03.034|last1=Langenhorst|first1=Malte|last2=Schumann|first2=Martin F.|last3=Paetel|first3=Stefan|last4=Schmager|first4=Raphael|last5=Lemmer|first5=Uli|last6=Richards|first6=Bryce S.|last7=Wegener|first7=Martin|last8=Paetzold|first8=Ulrich W.|bibcode=2018SEMSC.182..294L |s2cid=102944355}}</ref>

== Manufacture ==
{{more citations needed section|date=June 2014}}
[[File:FX-77.JPG|thumb|upright|Early [[solar-powered calculator]]]]

Solar cells share some processing and manufacturing techniques with other semiconductor devices and are commonly used in calculators, watches, and to power satellites in space.<ref>{{cite web |title=How Do Solar Panels Work? |url=https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/how-does-solar-work |website=energy.gov |date=3 December 2019 |publisher=US Department of Energy |access-date=4 June 2025}}</ref> However, the strict requirements for cleanliness and quality control of semiconductor fabrication are more relaxed for solar cells, lowering costs.

[[Polycrystalline silicon]] wafers are made by wire-sawing block-cast silicon ingots into 180 to 350 micrometer thick wafers. The wafers are usually lightly [[p-type semiconductor|p-type]]-doped. A surface diffusion of [[n-type semiconductor|n-type]] dopants is performed on the front side of the wafer. This forms a p–n junction a few hundred nanometers below the surface.

[[Anti-reflective coating|Anti-reflection coatings]] are then typically applied to increase the amount of light coupled into the solar cell. [[Silicon nitride]] has gradually replaced titanium dioxide as the preferred material, because of its excellent surface passivation qualities. It prevents carrier recombination at the cell surface. A layer several hundred nanometers thick is applied using [[plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition]]. Some solar cells have textured front surfaces that, like anti-reflection coatings, increase the amount of light reaching the wafer. Such surfaces were first applied to single-crystal silicon, followed by multicrystalline silicon somewhat later.

A full area metal contact is made on the back surface, and a grid-like metal contact made up of fine "fingers" and larger "bus bars" are screen-printed onto the front surface using a [[silver]] paste. This is an evolution of the so-called "wet" process for applying electrodes, first described in a US patent filed in 1981 by [[Bayer AG]].<ref>Fitzky, Hans G. and Ebneth, Harold (24 May 1983) {{US patent|4385102}}, "Large-area photovoltaic cell"</ref> The rear contact is formed by screen-printing a metal paste. To maximize frontal surface area available for sunlight and improve solar cell efficiency, manufacturers use various rear contact electrode techniques:

* Passivated emitter rear contact (PERC) uses a solid aluminum rear contact surface and adds a polymer film to capture light<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=TOPCon Solar Cells: The New PV Module Technology in the Solar Industry |url=https://solarmagazine.com/solar-panels/topcon-solar-cells/ |access-date=2025-02-21 |website=Solar Magazine |language=en-US}}</ref>
* Tunnel oxide passivated contact (TOPCon) uses a grid pattern of increasingly smaller silver rear bus bars and adds an oxidation layer to the PERC film to capture more light<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chan |first=Keng Siew |date=2019-11-21 |title=What is a TOPCON solar cell? - |url=https://www.kschan.com/what-is-a-topcon-solar-cell/ |access-date=2025-02-21 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite patent|number=US11824136B2|title=Solar cell, manufacturing method thereof, and photovoltaic module|gdate=2023-11-21|invent1=Yu|invent2=Liu|invent3=Zhang|inventor1-first=Kun|inventor2-first=Changming|inventor3-first=Xinyu|url=https://patents.google.com/patent/US11824136B2/en}}</ref>
* Interdigitated back contact (IBC)<ref>{{Cite web |title=Solar Cell Technology BSF PERC TOPCON HJT IBC - Knowledge - DS New Energy |url=https://www.dsneg.com/info/solar-cell-technology-bsf-perc-topcon-hjt-ibc-59069656.html |access-date=2025-02-21 |website=www.dsneg.com |language=en}}</ref>

The paste is then fired at several hundred degrees Celsius to form metal electrodes in [[ohmic contact]] with the silicon. Some companies use an additional electroplating step to increase efficiency. After the metal contacts are made, the solar cells are interconnected by flat wires or metal ribbons, and assembled into [[photovoltaic module|modules]] or "solar panels". Solar panels have a sheet of [[tempered glass]] on the front, and a [[polymer]] or glass encapsulation on the back.

Different types of manufacturing and recycling partly determine how effective it is in decreasing emissions and having a positive environmental effect.<ref name="techrev"/> Such differences and effectiveness could be quantified<ref name="techrev"/> for production of the most optimal types of products for different purposes in different regions across time.

=== Manufacturers and certification ===
{{Further|List of photovoltaics companies}}
{{Update section|date=November 2021}}
[[File:SolarCellProduction-E.PNG|thumb|upright=1.25|Solar cell production by region<ref name="pvnews">[http://www.greentechmedia.com/research/report/pv-news Pv News November 2012]. Greentech Media. Retrieved 3 June 2012.</ref>]]

In the United States, the [[National Renewable Energy Laboratory]] tests and validates solar technologies, with three groups certifying solar equipment alongside them: [[Underwriters Laboratories|UL]] and [[IEEE]] (both U.S. standards) and the [[International Electrotechnical Commission|IEC]]{{Citation needed|date=February 2024}}.

The [[International Energy Agency|IEA]]'s 2022 Special Report highlights China's dominance over the solar PV [[supply chain]], with an investment exceeding US$50 billion and the creation of around 300,000 jobs since 2011. China commands over 80% of all manufacturing stages for solar panels. This control has drastically cut costs but also led to issues like supply-demand imbalances and [[polysilicon]] production constraints. Nevertheless, China's strategic policies have reduced solar PV costs by more than 80%, increasing global affordability. In 2021, China's solar PV exports were over US$30 billion.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2022 |title=Special Report on Solar PV Global Supply Chains |url=https://iea.blob.core.windows.net/assets/d2ee601d-6b1a-4cd2-a0e8-db02dc64332c/SpecialReportonSolarPVGlobalSupplyChains.pdf |website=International Energy Agency}}</ref>

Meeting global energy and climate targets necessitates a major expansion in solar PV manufacturing, aiming for over 630 GW by 2030 according to the IEA's "Roadmap to Net Zero Emissions by 2050". China's dominance, controlling nearly 95% of key solar PV components and 40% of the world's polysilicon production in Xinjiang, poses risks of supply shortages and cost surges. Critical mineral demand, like silver, may exceed 30% of 2020's global production by 2030.<ref name=":0" />

In 2021, China's share of solar [[PV module]] production reached approximately 70%, an increase from 50% in 2010. Other key producers included Vietnam (5%), Malaysia (4%), Korea (4%), and Thailand (2%), with much of their production capacity developed by Chinese companies aimed at exports, notably to the United States.<ref name=":0" />

==== China ====
{{Main|Solar power in China}}
As of September 2018, sixty percent of the world's solar photovoltaic modules were made in China.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20180822-why-china-is-transforming-the-worlds-solar-energy|title=How China's giant solar farms are transforming world energy|last=Baraniuk|first=Chris|website=www.bbc.com|date=4 September 2018 |language=en|access-date=2019-10-24}}</ref> In 2021, China's share of solar [[PV module]] production reached approximately 70%.<ref name=":0" />

In 2022, China maintained its position as the world's largest PV module producer, holding a dominant market share of 77.8%. In the first half of 2023, China's production of PV modules exceeded 220 GW, marking an increase of over 62% compared to the same period in 2022.<ref name=":1" />

In 2025, China's PV manufacture sector faced an [[overproduction|overcapacity]] problem.<ref name=kenn>{{cite web |last1=Kennedy |first1=Charles |title=China Smashes Solar Installation Record |url=https://oilprice.com/Latest-Energy-News/World-News/China-Smashes-Solar-Installation-Record.html |website=OilPrice.com |language=en |date=23 June 2025}}</ref>

==== Vietnam ====
In 2022, Vietnam was the second-largest PV module producer, only behind China, with its production capacity rising to 24.1 GW, marking a significant 47% increase from the 16.4 GW produced in 2021. Vietnam further accounted for 6.4% of the world's photovoltaic module production in 2023.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2023|title=Trends in Photovoltaic Applications 2023|url=https://iea-pvps.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/PVPS_Trends_Report_2023_WEB.pdf|website=International Energy Agency}}</ref> However, this fell to 2.5% in 2025, making Vietnam the fourth-largest PV module producer worldwide.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://iea-pvps.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/IEA-PVPS_Trends_2025-.pdf|title=Trends in Photovoltaic Applications 2025|date=2025|access-date=January 29, 2026|website=International Energy Agency}}</ref>

==== Malaysia ====
{{main|Photovoltaics manufacturing in Malaysia}}

In 2022, Malaysia was the third-largest PV module producer, with a production capacity of 10.8 GW, accounting for 2.8% of global production. This placed it behind China, which dominated with 77.8%, and Vietnam, which contributed 6.4%.<ref name=":1" />

=== Materials sourcing ===
{{Expand section|date=November 2021}}
Like many other energy generation technologies, the manufacture of solar cells, especially its rapid expansion, has many environmental and supply-chain implications. Global mining may adapt and potentially expand for sourcing the needed minerals which vary per type of solar cell.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Herrington |first1=Richard |title=Mining our green future |journal=Nature Reviews Materials |date=June 2021 |volume=6 |issue=6 |pages=456–458 |doi=10.1038/s41578-021-00325-9 |bibcode=2021NatRM...6..456H |s2cid=235128115 |language=en |issn=2058-8437|doi-access=free }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Kemp |first1=Deanna |last2=Bainton |first2=Nick |title=More clean energy means more mines – we shouldn't sacrifice communities in the name of climate action |url=https://theconversation.com/more-clean-energy-means-more-mines-we-shouldnt-sacrifice-communities-in-the-name-of-climate-action-170938 |website=The Conversation |date=4 November 2021 |access-date=9 November 2021 |language=en}}</ref> [[#Recycling|Recycling]] solar panels could be a source for materials that would otherwise need to be mined.<ref name="techrev"/>

== Deployment ==
=== China ===
As of May 2018, the largest photovoltaic plant in the world is located in the Tengger desert in China.<ref name="ieefa.org">{{Cite web|url=https://ieefa.org/ieefa-report-advances-in-solar-energy-accelerate-global-shift-in-electricity-generation/|title=IEEFA Report: Advances in Solar Energy Accelerate Global Shift in Electricity Generation|date=2018-05-21|website=Institute for Energy Economics & Financial Analysis|language=en-US|access-date=2019-10-24}}</ref> In 2018, China added more photovoltaic installed capacity (in GW) than the next 9 countries combined.<ref name="iea-pvps.org">{{Cite web |url=http://www.iea-pvps.org/fileadmin/dam/public/report/statistics/IEA-PVPS_T1_35_Snapshot2019-Report.pdf |title=2019 Snapshot of Global PV Markets |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190421164151/http://www.iea-pvps.org/fileadmin/dam/public/report/statistics/IEA-PVPS_T1_35_Snapshot2019-Report.pdf |archive-date=2019-04-21}}</ref>

=== United States ===
{{Main|Solar power in the United States}}

Solar energy production in the U.S. doubled from 2013 to 2019.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://e360.yale.edu/digest/solar-energy-capacity-in-u-s-cities-has-doubled-in-the-last-6-years|title=Solar Energy Capacity in U.S. Cities Has Doubled in the Last 6 Years|website=Yale E360|language=en-US|access-date=2019-10-24}}</ref> This was driven first by the falling price of quality silicon,<ref>[https://cleantechnica.com/2013/03/07/plunging-cost-of-solar-pv-graphs/ Plunging Cost Of Solar PV (Graphs)]. CleanTechnica (7 March 2013). Retrieved 20 April 2014.</ref><ref>[http://www.downtoearth.org.in/content/falling-silicon-prices-shakes-solar-manufacturing-industry Falling silicon prices shakes up solar manufacturing industry] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131220202649/http://www.downtoearth.org.in/content/falling-silicon-prices-shakes-solar-manufacturing-industry |date=20 December 2013 }}. Down To Earth (19 September 2011). Retrieved 20 April 2014.</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.statista.com/statistics/301564/us-silicon-price-by-type/|title=Silicon price by type U.S. 2018|website=Statista|language=en|access-date=2019-10-24}}</ref> and later simply by the globally plunging cost of photovoltaic modules.<ref name="ieefa.org"/><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://news.energysage.com/solar-panel-efficiency-cost-over-time/|title=How Solar Panel Cost & Efficiency Change Over Time {{!}} EnergySage|date=2019-07-04|website=Solar News|language=en-US|access-date=2019-10-24}}</ref> In 2018, the U.S. added 10.8GW of installed solar photovoltaic energy, an increase of 21%.<ref name="iea-pvps.org"/>

=== Latin America ===

Latin America has emerged as a promising region for solar energy development in recent years, with over 10 GW of installations in 2020. The solar market in Latin America has been driven by abundant solar resources, falling costs, competitive auctions and growing electricity demand. Some of the leading countries for solar energy in Latin America are Brazil, Mexico, Chile and Argentina. However, the solar market in Latin America also faces some challenges, such as political instability, financing gaps and power transmission bottlenecks.<ref>IRENA (International Renewable Energy Agency). Renewable Energy Statistics 2022. IRENA. 2022.</ref>

=== Middle East and Africa ===

The Middle East and Africa have also experienced significant growth in solar energy deployment in recent years, with over 8 GW installations in 2020. The solar market in the Middle East and Africa has been driven by the low-cost generation of solar energy, the diversification of energy sources, the fight against climate change and rural electrification are motivated. Some of the notable countries for solar energy in the Middle East and Africa are Saudi Arabia, United Arab Emirates, Egypt, Morocco and South Africa. However, the solar market in the Middle East and Africa also faces several obstacles, including social unrest, regulatory uncertainty and technical barriers.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-11-02 |title=Weltweite Solarenergiemärkte: Wachstum, Trends und Herausforderungen - Arbitrage Solar |url=https://arbitragesolar.de/weltweite-solarenergiemarkte-wachstum-trends-und-herausforderungen/ |access-date=2023-11-02 |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Disposal ==
Solar cells degrade over time and lose their efficiency. Solar cells in extreme climates, such as desert or polar, are more prone to degradation due to exposure to harsh UV light and snow loads respectively.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Jordan |first1=Dirk C. |last2= Kurtz |first2= Sarah R. |author2-link= Sarah Kurtz |date= June 2012 |title= Photovoltaic Degradation Rates – An Analytical Review |url= https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy12osti/51664.pdf |journal=Progress in Photovoltaics: Research and Applications |access-date= 6 March 2019}}</ref> Usually, solar panels are given a lifespan of 25–30 years before decommissioning.<ref>[https://news.energysage.com/how-long-do-solar-panels-last/ How long do solar panels last?]. CleanTechnica (4 February 2019). Retrieved 6 March 2019.</ref>

The International Renewable Energy Agency estimated that the amount of solar panel [[electronic waste]] generated in 2016 was 43,500–250,000 metric tons. This number is estimated to increase substantially by 2030, reaching an estimated waste volume of 60–78 million metric tons in 2050.<ref>[https://www.irena.org/-/media/Files/IRENA/Agency/Publication/2016/IRENA_IEAPVPS_End-of-Life_Solar_PV_Panels_2016.pdf End-of-Life Management: Solar Photovoltaic Panels]. International Renewable Energy Agency (June 2016). Retrieved 6 March 2019.</ref>

=== Recycling ===
{{See also|Perovskite solar cell#Recycling}}
The most widely used solar cells in the market are crystalline solar cells. A product is truly recyclable if it can be harvested again. In the [[Paris Agreement|2016 Paris Agreement]], 195 countries agreed to reduce their carbon emissions by shifting their focus away from fossil fuels and towards renewable energy sources. Owing to this, Solar will be a major contributor to electricity generation all over the world. So, there will be a plethora of solar panels to be recycled after the end of their life cycle. In fact, many researchers around the globe have voiced their concern about finding ways to use silicon cells after recycling.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Doi |first1=Takuya |last2=Tsuda |first2=Izumi |last3=Unagida |first3=Hiroaki |last4=Murata |first4=Akinobu |last5=Sakuta |first5=Koichi |last6=Kurokawa |first6=Kosuke |date=March 2001 |title=Experimental study on PV module recycling with organic solvent method |journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells |volume=67 |issue=1–4 |pages=397–403 |doi=10.1016/s0927-0248(00)00308-1 |bibcode=2001SEMSC..67..397D |issn=0927-0248}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Yamashita |first1=Katsuya |last2=Miyazawa |first2=Akira |last3=Sannomiya |first3=Hitoshi |title=2006 IEEE 4th World Conference on Photovoltaic Energy Conference |chapter=Reserch and Development on Recycling and Reuse Treatment Technologies for Crystalline Silicon Photovoltaic Modules |date=2006 |pages=2254–2257 |chapter-url=http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/wcpec.2006.279621 |publisher=IEEE |doi=10.1109/wcpec.2006.279621|isbn=1-4244-0016-3 |s2cid=933430 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Micheli |first1=Leonardo |last2=Fernández |first2=Eduardo F. |last3=Muller |first3=Matthew |last4=Smestad |first4=Greg P. |last5=Almonacid |first5=Florencia |date=August 2020 |title=Selection of optimal wavelengths for optical soiling modelling and detection in photovoltaic modules |journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells |volume=212 |article-number=110539 |doi=10.1016/j.solmat.2020.110539 |s2cid=218900846 |issn=0927-0248|arxiv=2005.13020 |bibcode=2020SEMSC.21210539M |hdl=11573/1625572 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Eberspacher |first1=C. |last2=Fthenakis |first2=V.M.  |title=Conference Record of the Twenty Sixth IEEE Photovoltaic Specialists Conference − 1997 |chapter=Disposal and recycling of end-of-life PV modules |date=1997 |pages=1067–1072 |chapter-url=http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/pvsc.1997.654272 |publisher=IEEE |doi=10.1109/pvsc.1997.654272|isbn=0-7803-3767-0 |s2cid=118374147 }}</ref>

Additionally, these cells have hazardous elements/compounds, including [[lead]] (Pb), [[cadmium]] (Cd) or cadmium sulfide (CdS), selenium (Se), and barium (Ba) as dopants aside from the valuables silicon (Si), aluminum (Al), silver (Ag), and copper (Cu). The harmful elements/compounds if not disposed of with the proper technique can have severe harmful effects on human life and wildlife alike.<ref name="Rahman">{{Cite journal |last1=Rahman |first1=Md Mokhlesur |last2=Mateti |first2=Srikanth |last3=Sultana |first3=Irin |last4=Hou |first4=Chunping |last5=Falin |first5=Alexey |last6=Cizek |first6=Pavel |last7=Glushenkov |first7=Alexey M. |last8=Chen |first8=Ying |date=2021-05-05 |title=End-of-Life Photovoltaic Recycled Silicon: A Sustainable Circular Materials Source for Electronic Industries |journal=Advanced Energy and Sustainability Research |volume=2 |issue=11 |article-number=2100081 |doi=10.1002/aesr.202100081 |s2cid=235568140 |issn=2699-9412|doi-access=free |bibcode=2021AdESR...200081R |hdl=10536/DRO/DU:30152718 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

There are various ways c-Si can be recycled. Mainly thermal and chemical separation methods are used. This happens in two stages<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Klugmann-Radziemska |first1=Ewa |last2=Ostrowski |first2=Piotr |last3=Drabczyk |first3=Kazimierz |last4=Panek |first4=Piotr |last5=Szkodo |first5=Marek |date=December 2010 |title=Experimental validation of crystalline silicon solar cells recycling by thermal and chemical methods |journal=Solar Energy Materials and Solar Cells |volume=94 |issue=12 |pages=2275–2282 |doi=10.1016/j.solmat.2010.07.025 |bibcode=2010SEMSC..94.2275K |issn=0927-0248}}</ref>
* PV solar cell separation: in thermal delamination, the [[Ethylene-vinyl acetate|ethylene vinyl acetate]] (EVA) is removed and materials such as glass, Tedlar®, aluminium frame, steel, copper and plastics are separated;
* cleansing the surface of PV solar cells: unwanted layers (antireflection layer, metal coating and p–n semiconductor) are removed from the silicon solar cells separated from the PV modules; as a result, the silicon substrate, suitable for re-use, can be recovered.

The first solar panel recycling plant opened in Rousset, France in 2018. It was set to recycle 1300 tonnes of solar panel waste a year, and can increase its capacity to 4000 tonnes.<ref>If Solar Panels Are So Clean, Why Do They Produce So Much Toxic Waste?. Forbes (23 May 2018). Retrieved 6 March 2019.</ref><ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/us-solar-recycling/europes-first-solar-panel-recycling-plant-opens-in-france-idUSKBN1JL28Z Europe's First Solar Panel Recycling Plant Opens in France]. Reuters (25 June 2018). Retrieved 6 March 2019.</ref><ref>[https://elecsome.com/ solar panel upcycling solutions in Australia]. Retrieved 30 November 2019.</ref> If recycling is driven only by market-based prices, rather than also environmental regulations, the economic incentives for recycling remain uncertain and as of 2021 the environmental impact of different types of developed recycling techniques still need to be quantified.<ref name="techrev" />

